traversal.max_traversers is an ordinary g.with() option, checked once per step boundary (a counter comparison, no per-item work). A single step can overshoot the cap by one expansion, since the check runs when the step has produced its output. A cross product (a v() in the middle of a traversal, as in g.v().v() or the GQL MATCH (a), (b)) is checked before it is built: its size is known up front, so a product over the cap fails at once instead of allocating it first. A count that comes from the query (a bulk, a row count, the size of a product) never becomes an unchecked up-front reservation. Where the engine reserves an exact size it asks the allocator fallibly: when the system cannot provide that block (the limit raised to "unlimited" and a product of trillions of rows), the run fails with ResourceLimitExceeded of memory.allocation and is rolled back instead of the process aborting. memory.allocation is not an option: narrow the query, or bound the run with the memory budget . g.with("traversal.max_traversers", 50000000).v().out().out().count() A host sets a default and locks it with an ExecutionPolicy, exactly like evaluationTimeout: let policy = ExecutionPolicy::permissive() .with_default("traversal.max_traversers", 1_000_000) .with_locked("traversal.max_traversers"); Every traversal limit is an administrative option: a script's own g.with(..) of it asks the host's authorizer for Update on Option(key), while a host's preset (graph_scope_with_options) or policy default asks nothing. The presets of graph_scope_with_options reach every traversal source of the session: g, _g and __ (also a traversal started from __, such as __.inject(1).repeat(..)). Build the engine with engine_with_options(&limits, authorizer, &options) and the same options, so the sources a script creates itself (GraphSource::empty(), GraphSource::tinkerpop_modern(), schema.toGraph(), ...) get them too; otherwise a script could escape a preset evaluationTimeout by building its own source. A gql() query is bounded by the same options. An unbounded quantified edge (-[:knows]->{1,}, *, +, Cypher [*]) or quantified path pattern (((x)-[:knows]->(y)-[:created]->(z))+) has no limit of its own: trail matching ends it on a finite graph, and repeat.max_loops (counting iterations of the whole body) and traversal.max_traversers turn a walk that grows too far into an error, never into a shortened result. The help of that error names the quantified edge or path pattern, suggests an upper bound and shows g.with(..).gql(..) with a raised limit ( GQL Semantics ): g.with("repeat.max_loops", 1).gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[:knows|created]->*(b) RETURN b.name AS b") // error: repeat() exceeded the loop limit of 1 iterations
g.with("repeat.max_loops", 5).gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[:knows|created]->*(b) RETURN count(*) AS n").to_list() // #{"n": 6}
