Experimental. GQL support is new in Graphersal 0.2.2 and marked experimental: its subset grows from version to version, and the answers of a construct (its rows, errors and plans) may still change in a later version. Gremlin stays the complete, stable interface: a gql() query runs as ordinary Gremlin steps, and this chapter shows the Gremlin twin of each construct. g.gql(query) runs a query written in GQL , the ISO standard graph query language (ISO/IEC 39075), on the same graph and the same engine as Gremlin. A GQL query describes the pattern you are looking for; Graphersal finds every match and returns one row per match: g.gql("MATCH (p:person)-[:created]->(s:software) WHERE p.age > 30 RETURN p.name AS who, s.name AS what ORDER BY who, what").to_list()
// #{"what": "lop", "who": "josh"}, #{"what": "ripple", "who": "josh"}, #{"what": "lop", "who": "peter"} The Gremlin equivalent walks the graph step by step: g.v().has_label("person").has("age", P.gt(30)).as("p").out("created").has_label("software").as("s").select("p", "s").by("name").to_list()
// #{"p": "josh", "s": "ripple"}, #{"p": "josh", "s": "lop"}, #{"p": "peter", "s": "lop"} Every example in this chapter was run on the sample graph modern; the comment after // is its real output (a list is shown as its items, comma-separated, or one per line).

