ISO GQL is the norm. Queries are written in GQL; where GQL and Cypher differ, GQL wins. Familiar Cypher spellings are accepted as aliases where they are unambiguous and mean the same (SKIP for OFFSET, != for <>, toUpper() for upper(), --> for ->, ...); Cypher Aliases lists every one. There is no Graphersal-only syntax: an accepted query means the same in a GQL or Cypher 25 database (modulo the listed aliases). Reads and writes. INSERT (Cypher CREATE) creates nodes and edges, SET and REMOVE change their properties and labels, and DELETE / DETACH DELETE remove them, each once per row, and Cypher's MERGE finds or creates a pattern ( Writing with GQL ). A query is one unit: a query that fails changes nothing. A start step. gql() starts a traversal: g.gql(..), never g.v().gql(..) or __.gql(..). Rows are maps. Each result row is a map from column name to value ( Result Shape ). RETURN is mandatory unless the query ends in a write. One engine. The query is compiled into an ordinary traversal plan: the same optimizer, property indexes, profile(), limits, cancellation, permissions and storages as Gremlin ( Plans and Performance ). A query that does not parse fails before anything runs, with a caret under the offending text ( Errors ). This version supports INSERT and GQL read queries: MATCH and OPTIONAL MATCH, WHERE, LET, FILTER, Cypher's WITH, RETURN with aggregation (count, sum, avg, min, max, collect_list), ORDER BY, OFFSET, LIMIT, FOR (Cypher UNWIND) with a direct lookup per item where an id or index serves it, queries that start without MATCH (RETURN 1 + 2 AS x), EXISTS subqueries and Cypher pattern predicates (WHERE NOT (p)-[:created]->()), Cypher map projections and list and pattern comprehensions for nested, JSON-shaped answers (p {.name, friends: [(p)-[:knows]->(f) | f.name]}), Cypher's COUNT { } / COLLECT { } and list predicates (all(), any(), reduce(), ...), composite queries (UNION, UNION ALL, NEXT), CALL { } subqueries run per row (top k per group), node and edge patterns, quantified edges (-[:knows]->{1,3}), quantified path patterns that repeat a whole chain ((a) ((x)-[:created]->(s)<-[:created]-(y)){1,2} (b)), path variables, label expressions, expressions, parameters, the GQL scalar functions and the common openCypher ones (toInteger, substring, split, round, keys, nodes(p), ...) ( The Supported Subset ). EXCEPT / INTERSECT, shortest paths and a quantifier nested inside a quantified path pattern come later; until then the error names the form or the Gremlin step that does the same. g.gql("MATCH (p:person) OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) RETURN p.name AS name, count(s) AS made ORDER BY made DESC, name").to_list()
// josh 2, marko 1, peter 1, vadas 0
