A quantifier after an edge pattern repeats the edge: (a)-[:knows]->{1,3}(b) matches a path of one to three knows edges from a to b, every node in between unnamed. It applies to full and abbreviated edges (->{2}) in every direction. Quantifier Repeats the edge Cypher spelling ( alias ) {m,n} m to n times [*m..n] {n} exactly n times [*n] {m,} at least m times [*m..] {,n} 0 to n times * 0 or more times, {0,} + 1 or more times, {1,} [*] (Cypher's lower bound is 1) 1 to n times, {1,n} [*..n] never: no match [*m..n] with m > n, [*..0] A GQL lower bound above the upper bound ({3,1}) is a syntax error; the Cypher spelling of the same interval ([*3..1]) is empty and matches nothing, as in Neo4j. A lower bound of 0 includes the zero-length match, where b is a itself. The edges of one match are all different (trail), so an unbounded quantifier ends on every finite graph ( Semantics ). The Gremlin twin is a repeat() with emit() and times(): g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[:knows|created]->{1,2}(b) RETURN b.name AS b ORDER BY b").to_list()
// josh, lop, lop, ripple, vadas
g.v().has("name", "marko").repeat(__.out("knows", "created")).emit().times(2).values("name").order().to_list()
// "josh", "lop", "lop", "ripple", "vadas"
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})->{2}(b) RETURN b.name AS b ORDER BY b").to_list() // #{"b": "lop"}, #{"b": "ripple"}
g.v().has("name", "marko").repeat(__.out()).times(2).values("name").order().to_list() // "lop", "ripple"
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})->{0,1}(b) RETURN b.name AS b ORDER BY b").to_list() // josh, lop, marko, vadas
g.v().has("name", "marko").emit().repeat(__.out()).times(1).values("name").order().to_list() // "josh", "lop", "marko", "vadas"
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[:knows]->{1,2}(b)-[:created]->(s) RETURN b.name AS b, s.name AS s ORDER BY b, s").to_list()
// #{"b": "josh", "s": "lop"}, #{"b": "josh", "s": "ripple"} The variable of a quantified edge is a group variable : after the pattern it is the list of the edges of the match, in the written order ([] for a zero-length match). Inside its own brackets (-[e WHERE e.weight > 0.5]->{1,2}) it is the single edge of each repetition, so the condition tests every edge: g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[e]->*(b) RETURN b.name AS b, size(e) AS hops ORDER BY hops, b").to_list()
// marko 0, josh 1, lop 1, vadas 1, lop 2, ripple 2
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[e:knows|created WHERE e.weight > 0.5]->{1,2}(b) RETURN b.name AS b, e AS edges ORDER BY b").to_list()
// #{"b": "josh", "edges": [e[2][1-knows->4]]}, #{"b": "ripple", "edges": [e[2][1-knows->4], e[4][4-created->5]]}
g.v().has("name", "marko").repeat(__.out_e("knows", "created").has("weight", P.gt(0.5)).in_v()).emit().times(2).values("name").order().to_list()
// "josh", "ripple" To repeat more than one edge, put the chain in parentheses and quantify it: a quantified path pattern .

