A parenthesized path pattern ( <chain> [WHERE <condition>] ) with a quantifier after its ) repeats the whole chain, its nodes, edges and conditions included: (a) ((x)-[:created]->(s)<-[:created]-(y)){1,2} (b) walks from a to b over one or two "created the same software" hops. It takes every quantifier of a quantified edge ({m,n}, {n}, {m,}, {,n}, *, +) and also ? (0 or 1 times); without a quantifier it matches exactly once. The outer nodes merge with the body. The node written before the group is the first node of the first iteration, the node after it the last node of the last iteration, and consecutive iterations join end to start: (a) ((x)-->(y)){1,3} (b) binds a = the first x and b = the last y. Where the text has no node next to the group (at the start or end of the path, or between two groups), an anonymous one stands. After zero iterations ({0,n}, *, ?) the two outer nodes are the same node. Body variables are group variables. With a quantifier other than ?, every variable inside the body is, after the pattern, the list of its nodes or edges, one per iteration in walk order, like the variable of a quantified edge. Read the list (size(x), [n IN x | n.name], RETURN x), not a property of it: x.name outside the body is an error. With ? or without a quantifier the body variables are single values; with ? they are NULL when the part matched nothing ( Semantics ). The body condition runs in every iteration. The WHERE before the closing parenthesis, and the inline maps and WHEREs of the body's elements, test each iteration's elements, and read only the body's variables, parameters and values; test an outer variable in the WHERE of the MATCH clause. Trail holds across iterations. No edge is bound twice in one match, across the iterations, the body's edges and the other edges of the clause. g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'}) ((x)-[e:knows|created]->(y)){1,2} (b) RETURN b.name AS b, size(e) AS hops ORDER BY hops, b").to_list()
// josh 1, lop 1, vadas 1, lop 2, ripple 2
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'}) ((x)-[:knows|created]->(y)){1,2} (b) RETURN [n IN x | n.name] AS xs, [n IN y | n.name] AS ys ORDER BY ys").to_list()
// #{"xs": ["marko"], "ys": ["josh"]}, #{"xs": ["marko", "josh"], "ys": ["josh", "lop"]}, #{"xs": ["marko", "josh"], "ys": ["josh", "ripple"]}, #{"xs": ["marko"], "ys": ["lop"]}, #{"xs": ["marko"], "ys": ["vadas"]}
g.gql("MATCH (a:person) ((x)-[:created]->(s)<-[:created]-(y)){1,2} (b) RETURN DISTINCT a.name AS a, b.name AS b ORDER BY a, b").to_list()
// josh/marko, josh/peter, marko/josh, marko/peter, peter/josh, peter/marko
g.gql("MATCH (a:person) ((x)-[:knows|created]-(y) WHERE y.age > x.age){1,3} (b) RETURN a.name AS a, b.name AS b, size(y) AS steps ORDER BY a, b").to_list()
// #{"a": "marko", "b": "josh", "steps": 1}, #{"a": "vadas", "b": "josh", "steps": 2}, #{"a": "vadas", "b": "marko", "steps": 1}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'josh'}) ((x)-[:created]->(s) WHERE s.lang = 'java')? (q) RETURN s.name AS project, q.name AS last ORDER BY project").to_list()
// #{"last": "lop", "project": "lop"}, #{"last": "ripple", "project": "ripple"}, #{"last": "josh", "project": ()}
g.gql("MATCH ((x:person)-[:created]->(s) WHERE s.name = 'ripple') RETURN x.name AS who").to_list() // #{"who": "josh"} The Gremlin twin is a repeat() of the body's steps; the second-to-last query is a filter on the body run in every iteration: g.v().has_label("person").as("a").repeat(__.out_e("created").in_v().in_e("created").out_v()).emit().times(2).simple_path().as("b").select("a", "b").by("name").dedup().to_list()
// the same six pairs (simple_path() stands in for trail here) A quantified path pattern stands wherever a pattern does: in MATCH and OPTIONAL MATCH, in EXISTS { }, COUNT { } and COLLECT { }, in a pattern comprehension and in a Cypher pattern predicate (which names no new variable, so its body stays anonymous): g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS p, [(p) ((x)-[:created]->(s)<-[:created]-(y)){1,2} (q) | q.name] AS co ORDER BY p").to_list()
// josh [peter, marko], marko [peter, josh], peter [josh, marko], vadas []
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE EXISTS { (p) ((x)-[:knows]->(y)){1,3} (q:person {name: 'josh'}) } RETURN p.name AS p").to_list() // #{"p": "marko"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE (p) (()-[:knows]->()-[:created]->()){1} RETURN p.name AS p").to_list() // #{"p": "marko"} Not yet supported inside the body (each refused with GQLSTATUS 0A000 and a help line naming the rewrite): a nested parenthesized path pattern, a quantified edge, a path variable (p = goes on the whole path), a path mode or search prefix, several comma-separated patterns or a union (|), a body without an edge, a variable written twice in one body, a variable bound outside the body written again inside it, a condition reading an outer variable, and a subquery. A variable inside a quantified body must be a new name (42002). g.gql("MATCH (a) (((x)-->(y)){2})+ (b) RETURN b") // error: a parenthesized path pattern nested in another one is not yet supported
g.gql("MATCH (a) ((x)-->(y)-->(z)){1,3} (b) RETURN b.name AS b ORDER BY b").to_list() // #{"b": "lop"}, #{"b": "ripple"}
