An EXISTS subquery tests whether a pattern matches: it is TRUE when its body has at least one match for the row and FALSE otherwise, never NULL. Three forms mean the same: EXISTS { MATCH <pattern> [, <pattern> ...] [WHERE <condition>] [MATCH ...] }
EXISTS { <pattern> [, <pattern> ...] [WHERE <condition>] }
EXISTS ( ... ) -- either body in parentheses
(a)-[:knows]->(b) -- Cypher pattern predicate: a pattern where a condition stands The body reads the variables bound before it (they are imported : p inside is the row's p), and the variables it binds itself stay inside it. A pattern predicate is EXISTS { <pattern> } written without the keyword; it must not name a new variable (write EXISTS { MATCH .. } to name one), and it stands only where a condition does: a WHERE or FILTER condition (also under NOT, AND, OR, XOR), the WHERE of a comprehension and a WHEN of a searched CASE. Anywhere else (a RETURN column, a function argument, a LET value) it is a syntax error (42001), as in Neo4j: write EXISTS { .. }, COUNT { .. } or a pattern comprehension there. The Gremlin twins are where(__..) and not(__..), and the "who has no ..." question runs exactly as its twin: g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE NOT EXISTS { (p)-[:created]->() } RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // #{"name": "vadas"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE NOT (p)-[:created]->() RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // #{"name": "vadas"}
g.v().has_label("person").not(__.out("created")).values("name").to_list() // "vadas"
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE EXISTS { MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) WHERE s.name = 'ripple' } RETURN p.name AS name").to_list() // #{"name": "josh"}
g.v().has_label("person").where(__.out("created").has("name", "ripple")).values("name").to_list() // "josh"
g.gql("MATCH (a:person), (b:person) WHERE (a)-[:knows]->(b) RETURN a.name AS a, b.name AS b ORDER BY b").to_list()
// #{"a": "marko", "b": "josh"}, #{"a": "marko", "b": "vadas"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE EXISTS (MATCH (p)-[:knows]->(f) MATCH (f)-[:created]->(:software {name: 'ripple'})) RETURN p.name AS name").to_list() // #{"name": "marko"} EXISTS stands anywhere an expression does: in WHERE and FILTER, in the inline WHERE of a node or edge pattern, under OR, XOR and CASE, in a RETURN or WITH column, an ORDER BY key, a grouping key or an aggregate's argument, a FOR list. It composes with three-valued logic like any boolean ( Semantics ). g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE p.age > 30 OR (p)<-[:knows]-() RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // josh, peter, vadas
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS name, EXISTS { (p)-[:knows]->() } AS knows ORDER BY knows DESC, name").to_list()
// #{"knows": true, "name": "marko"}, #{"knows": false, "name": "josh"}, ... peter, vadas
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN EXISTS { (p)-[:created]->() } AS creator, count(*) AS n ORDER BY creator").to_list()
// #{"creator": false, "n": 1}, #{"creator": true, "n": 3}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person WHERE NOT (p)-[:created]->())-[:knows]-(f) RETURN p.name AS p, f.name AS f").to_list() // #{"f": "marko", "p": "vadas"} The body holds MATCH clauses with their WHERE (and nested subqueries), or a whole query : any clauses (OPTIONAL MATCH, WITH, LET, FILTER, FOR, CALL), UNION and NEXT, ending in RETURN. EXISTS is then TRUE when that query returns at least one row, and its RETURN items are still evaluated (a failing expression there fails the query). Whole-query bodies nest at most 16 levels, CALL bodies included. A query holds at most 64 subqueries, pattern comprehensions , COUNT { } and COLLECT { } included ( Errors ). g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE EXISTS { MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) WITH count(s) AS c WHERE c > 1 RETURN c } RETURN p.name AS name").to_list() // #{"name": "josh"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE EXISTS { MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) RETURN s UNION MATCH (p)-[:knows]->(s) RETURN s } RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // josh, marko, peter
