OPTIONAL MATCH keeps every row that reaches it: where its pattern (and its own WHERE) matches, the row is extended once per match; where it does not, the row continues once with the new variables set to NULL. Gremlin's twin is optional(). g.gql("MATCH (p:person) OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) RETURN p.name AS name, s.name AS sw ORDER BY name, sw").to_list()
// #{"name": "josh", "sw": "lop"}, #{"name": "josh", "sw": "ripple"}, #{"name": "marko", "sw": "lop"}, #{"name": "peter", "sw": "lop"}, #{"name": "vadas", "sw": ()}
g.v().has_label("person").as("p").optional(__.out("created").as("s")).project("name", "sw").by(__.select("p").values("name")).by(__.coalesce(__.select("s").values("name"), __.constant("-"))).order().by("name").to_list()
// the same five rows, vadas with "-" The WHERE of an OPTIONAL MATCH belongs to it: a row whose optional part fails the condition keeps its NULLs instead of being dropped. A leading OPTIONAL MATCH starts from one empty row, so a pattern without a match gives one row of NULLs: g.gql("MATCH (p:person) OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) WHERE s.lang = 'none' RETURN p.name AS name, s AS s ORDER BY name").to_list()
// josh, marko, peter, vadas, each with s ()
g.gql("OPTIONAL MATCH (n:robot) RETURN n").to_list() // #{"n": ()} NULL columns then work as everywhere: count(s) skips them, so this counts each person's software, 0 for vadas ( Aggregation ).

