WITH (Cypher; GQL's linear statements express the same) ends one part of the query and passes only its items on: the next clauses see only the names WITH lists (WITH * keeps all; with nothing in scope it passes the rows on, as in MATCH () CREATE () WITH * CREATE ()). An item that is not a plain variable needs AS. WITH may take DISTINCT, an aggregate, ORDER BY, OFFSET / SKIP, LIMIT and a WHERE, in this order; the WHERE filters after LIMIT (Cypher's order of the sub-clauses). The WHERE reads the items and also the variables the WITH drops (WITH c WHERE r IS NULL), except after an aggregation; after DISTINCT it filters the rows before they are folded (with DISTINCT and ORDER BY / OFFSET / LIMIT too, such a WHERE is not yet supported). g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WITH p.name AS name, p.age AS age WHERE age < 30 RETURN name ORDER BY name").to_list() // #{"name": "marko"}, #{"name": "vadas"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WITH p ORDER BY p.age DESC LIMIT 2 MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) RETURN p.name AS name, s.name AS sw ORDER BY name, sw").to_list()
// #{"name": "josh", "sw": "lop"}, #{"name": "josh", "sw": "ripple"}, #{"name": "peter", "sw": "lop"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WITH p ORDER BY p.age LIMIT 3 WHERE p.age > 28 RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list()
// #{"name": "josh"}, #{"name": "marko"}: the three youngest, then the filter
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[e:created]->() WITH p.name AS name WHERE e IS NULL RETURN name").to_list() // #{"name": "vadas"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WITH p.name AS name RETURN p.age") // error: the variable `p` is not in scope: the WITH before does not carry it
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WITH p ORDER BY p.age LIMIT 2 WITH collect(p) AS ps WITH ps[0] AS youngest MATCH (youngest)<-[:knows]-(f) RETURN f.name AS name").to_list() // #{"name": "marko"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WITH collect(p.age) AS ages WITH ages[0] AS x MATCH (x)-->(y) RETURN y") // error: `x` is written as a node of the pattern but holds a value that is not a node A WITH that only renames and computes values is as transparent as LET. One with DISTINCT, an aggregate, ORDER BY, OFFSET or LIMIT builds its rows first; a later MATCH continues from the elements in its columns. A LET or WITH value computed from elements (coalesce(b, c), l[0], head(collect(e))) may stand for a node or an edge of a later pattern, also as an endpoint of INSERT / CREATE: NULL matches nothing, a value of another kind is a data error (22G03) when a row reaches the pattern (a value written as an edge is compared with the edges the pattern walks, so one of another kind matches nothing). A value written as a literal, an operator or a property access is never an element and is refused before the run. The order a WITH ... ORDER BY makes is not kept by later clauses: sort again in the final RETURN ( Ordering ).

