FOR x IN list gives one row per item of the list, with the item bound to x; Cypher's UNWIND list AS x is the same clause ( Cypher Aliases ). The list is any expression over the row before the FOR: a literal, a $parameter, a property, a collect_list() column of a WITH, nodes(p). Duplicates and NULL items give rows like any other item; a list inside the list stays one item. WITH ORDINALITY i also binds the item's position from 1, WITH OFFSET i from 0 (only after FOR). Gremlin's twin is unfold(). g.gql("FOR x IN [1, 2, 2] RETURN x ORDER BY x").to_list() // #{"x": 1}, #{"x": 2}, #{"x": 2}
g.inject([1, 2, 2]).unfold().order().to_list() // 1, 2, 2
g.gql("UNWIND [1, 2, 2] AS x RETURN x ORDER BY x").to_list() // the same rows
g.gql("FOR x IN [10, 20] WITH ORDINALITY i RETURN x, i ORDER BY i").to_list() // #{"i": 1, "x": 10}, #{"i": 2, "x": 20}
g.gql("FOR x IN [10, 20] WITH OFFSET i RETURN x, i ORDER BY i").to_list() // #{"i": 0, "x": 10}, #{"i": 1, "x": 20}
g.gql("FOR x IN [1, 2] FOR y IN [x, x * 10] RETURN x, y ORDER BY x, y").to_list() // 1 1, 1 10, 2 2, 2 20 The most frequent use is "for each value in a list, find its vertex": an item compared in a property map or a WHERE equality. When an index or the element id serves that equality, each item is a direct lookup instead of a scan ( Plans and Performance ). A name without a vertex gives no row: g.gql("FOR n IN $names WITH ORDINALITY i MATCH (p:person {name: n}) RETURN i, n, p.age AS age ORDER BY i", #{names: ["vadas", "marko", "nobody"]}).to_list()
// #{"age": 27, "i": 1, "n": "vadas"}, #{"age": 29, "i": 2, "n": "marko"}
g.gql("FOR id IN $ids MATCH (n) WHERE element_id(n) = id RETURN id, n.name AS name ORDER BY id", #{ids: ["1", "3", "42"]}).to_list()
// #{"id": "1", "name": "marko"}, #{"id": "3", "name": "lop"} An item that is a vertex continues a pattern by its name, like a vertex column of a WITH (an item of another kind gives no row there): g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WITH collect_list(p) AS ps FOR p IN ps MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) RETURN p.name AS name, s.name AS sw ORDER BY name, sw").to_list()
// josh lop, josh ripple, marko lop, peter lop
g.gql("MATCH p = (a {name: 'marko'})-[:knows]->(b) FOR n IN nodes(p) MATCH (n)-[:created]->(s) RETURN DISTINCT n.name AS who, s.name AS sw ORDER BY who, sw").to_list()
// josh lop, josh ripple, marko lop A NULL list gives no row and a value that is not a list gives one row of that value ( Semantics ). The item and the position are new names: a name already in scope, or the same name for both, is an error.

