RETURN and WITH items may aggregate. The items without an aggregate are the grouping keys : one row per distinct combination of their values (grouping is implicit, as in Cypher). Without keys the whole input is one group. ISO GQL's GROUP BY may follow the RETURN items: it must list exactly the items without an aggregate (by column name, or by repeating the item's expression), changes nothing in the result, and GROUP BY () is the single group of an all-aggregate RETURN; without any aggregate it gives one row per group, like DISTINCT. After WITH (Cypher) GROUP BY is an error that explains implicit grouping. Aggregate Returns On no input count(*) the number of rows 0 count(x) the number of rows where x is not NULL 0 sum(x) the sum of the numbers; integers stay integers 0 avg(x) the mean, always a float NULL min(x), max(x) the smallest / largest value in the order of ORDER BY NULL collect_list(x) (Cypher collect(x)) the values as a list [] percentile_disc(x, p) (Cypher percentileDisc) the value at percentile p (0.0 to 1.0) of the sorted numbers, with its type NULL percentile_cont(x, p) (Cypher percentileCont) the percentile p interpolated linearly between the two nearest numbers, always a float NULL The percentiles take numbers (another kind is error 22G03) and a percentile p from 0.0 to 1.0 (outside it: error 22003), evaluated and checked on every row; the group uses the value of its first row, so write a constant or a $parameter. percentile_disc returns the value at position ceil(p * n) - 1 of the n sorted numbers (the first for p = 0), percentile_cont interpolates at position p * (n - 1) (Neo4j's definitions): g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN percentile_disc(p.age, 0.5) AS d, percentile_cont(p.age, 0.5) AS c, percentileCont(p.age, 1) AS top").to_list() // #{"c": 30.5, "d": 29, "top": 35.0}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN percentile_cont(p.age, 1.5) AS c") // error: percentile_cont() takes a percentile from 0.0 to 1.0, not 1.5 (22003) NULL values are skipped by every aggregate except count(*). DISTINCT inside the call (count(DISTINCT x)) aggregates each distinct value once. An aggregate may be part of an expression (count(*) + 1); outside its aggregates such an item reads only grouping keys, constants and parameters. It may repeat a key written as a variable or a property (p.age + count(*) beside the key p.age), not a computed key (a.age + b.age): compute that one in a WITH first, as in WITH *, a.age + b.age AS k RETURN k, k + count(*). The details are on Semantics . g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN count(*) AS n, sum(p.age) AS total, avg(p.age) AS mean, min(p.age) AS lo, max(p.age) AS hi").to_list()
// #{"hi": 35, "lo": 27, "mean": 30.75, "n": 4, "total": 123}
g.v().has_label("person").values("age").fold().project("n", "total", "mean", "lo", "hi").by(__.count(Scope.local)).by(__.sum(Scope.local)).by(__.mean(Scope.local)).by(__.min(Scope.local)).by(__.max(Scope.local)).to_list()
// the same values
g.gql("MATCH (s:software)<-[:created]-(p) RETURN s.name AS sw, count(p) AS authors ORDER BY sw").to_list()
// #{"authors": 3, "sw": "lop"}, #{"authors": 1, "sw": "ripple"}
g.v().has_label("software").group().by("name").by(__.in("created").count()).to_list() // #{"lop": 3, "ripple": 1}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person)-[:created]->(s) RETURN p.name AS name, collect_list(s.name) AS sw ORDER BY name").to_list()
// #{"name": "josh", "sw": ["ripple", "lop"]}, #{"name": "marko", "sw": ["lop"]}, #{"name": "peter", "sw": ["lop"]}
g.gql("MATCH (n) RETURN count(n.age) AS ages, count(*) AS all, count(DISTINCT n.lang) AS langs").to_list()
// #{"ages": 4, "all": 6, "langs": 1} With OPTIONAL MATCH, count() of an optional variable gives 0 where nothing matched; Gremlin counts per vertex with group() or project(): g.gql("MATCH (p:person) OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) RETURN p.name AS name, count(s) AS n ORDER BY name").to_list()
// josh 2, marko 1, peter 1, vadas 0
g.v().has_label("person").project("name", "n").by("name").by(__.out("created").count()).order().by("name").to_list()
// the same rows An aggregating WITH passes the groups on, and its WHERE filters them (SQL's HAVING): g.gql("MATCH (p:person)-[:created]->(s) WITH p, count(s) AS n WHERE n > 1 RETURN p.name AS name, n").to_list() // #{"n": 2, "name": "josh"}
