ORDER BY takes expressions (a column name, a property, a function call) with ASC / ASCENDING (the default) or DESC / DESCENDING; it may read variables that are not returned (except after RETURN DISTINCT). OFFSET n (Cypher SKIP n) skips rows, LIMIT n keeps at most n. They come in this order, each at most once; n is a non-negative integer literal, a parameter, or a constant expression: one without variables, aggregates or subqueries, evaluated once before the run (LIMIT 1 + 1, SKIP toInteger(ceil(1.7)); a negative result is an error 22G02, a non-integer one 22G03). An expression with a parameter in it (LIMIT $n + 1) is refused: pass the computed value as the parameter. g.gql("MATCH (n:person) RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY n.age DESC").to_list() // peter, josh, marko, vadas
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name OFFSET 1 LIMIT 2").to_list() // #{"name": "marko"}, #{"name": "peter"}
g.v().has_label("person").values("name").order().range(1, 3).to_list() // "marko", "peter"
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name OFFSET 2 - 1 LIMIT toInteger(ceil(1.7))").to_list() // #{"name": "marko"}, #{"name": "peter"}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) RETURN n.name AS name LIMIT n.age").to_list() // error: LIMIT ... not an expression that reads the row (22G03) Without ORDER BY the row order is unspecified; Semantics gives the order of NULL and of values of different types.

