Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) gives a language model the passages that answer a question. GraphRAG starts the same way, with the documents nearest to the question, and then follows the edges: who wrote them, what they are about, which other documents share the topic. Here each document has an author (wrote) and a topic (about). This block has no schema, so the lists are wrapped in vector(..): // Documents with toy embeddings [animals, food, technology], their authors and topics.
for d in [ ["Cats and dogs", "Ada", "pets", [0.9, 0.1, 0.0]], ["Feeding your cat", "Ada", "pets", [0.6, 0.7, 0.0]], ["Pasta at home", "Ben", "cooking", [0.0, 0.9, 0.1]], ["Robot chefs", "Cleo", "cooking", [0.1, 0.6, 0.8]], ["Rust for beginners", "Cleo", "programming", [0.0, 0.0, 1.0]],
] { g.merge_v(#{"T.label": "person", name: d[1]}).as("a") .merge_v(#{"T.label": "topic", name: d[2]}).as("t") .add_v("doc").property("title", d[0]).property("emb", vector(d[3])).as("d") .add_e("about").to("t") .add_e("wrote").from("a").to("d") .iterate();
} // "What should I cook for my cat?" ~ [0.5, 0.8, 0.0]: the two best hits with their context.
// [#{"author": "Ada", "title": "Feeding your cat", "topic": "pets"},
// #{"author": "Ben", "title": "Pasta at home", "topic": "cooking"}]
g.v().has_label("doc").nearest("emb", [0.5, 0.8, 0.0], 2) .project("title", "author", "topic") .by("title") .by(__.in("wrote").values("name")) .by(__.out("about").values("name")) .to_list() One more hop finds documents that a vector search alone would rank low, but that share the best hit's topic: for d in [ ["Cats and dogs", "pets", [0.9, 0.1, 0.0]], ["Feeding your cat", "pets", [0.6, 0.7, 0.0]], ["Pasta at home", "cooking", [0.0, 0.9, 0.1]], ["Rust for beginners", "programming", [0.0, 0.0, 1.0]],
] { g.merge_v(#{"T.label": "topic", name: d[1]}).as("t") .add_v("doc").property("title", d[0]).property("emb", vector(d[2])) .add_e("about").to("t") .iterate();
} // The best hit for [0.5, 0.8, 0.0] is "Feeding your cat"; its topic "pets" brings in:
// ["Cats and dogs"]
g.v().has_label("doc").nearest("emb", [0.5, 0.8, 0.0], 1).as("hit") .out("about").in("about").where(P.neq("hit")) .values("title").to_list() The host then turns the rows into the prompt for its language model. In Python this is a few lines ( below ).

