x[i] reads the item i of a list, counted from 0; a negative index counts from the end (x[-1] is the last item), and an index out of range gives NULL. x[a..b] is the sub-list from a up to b (excluded); either bound may be left out (x[..2], x[1..]), a negative one counts from the end, bounds beyond the list are clamped, and a >= b gives []. On a map m[k] reads the entry of the string key k, and on a node or an edge n[k] reads the property k; a missing key gives NULL. n['name'] with a string literal is the property access n.name (the same plan, so a property index serves it); a computed key (n[$key], n['na' || 'me']) is read per row. A NULL list, map, index or bound gives NULL. These are Cypher forms with their Neo4j meaning, measured against the openCypher TCK ( Semantics ). g.gql("RETURN [10, 20, 30][0] AS a, [10, 20, 30][-1] AS b, [10, 20, 30][5] AS c, [10, 20, 30][1..] AS d, [10, 20, 30][..-1] AS e").to_list()
// #{"a": 10, "b": 30, "c": (), "d": [20, 30], "e": [10, 20]}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WITH p ORDER BY p.age RETURN collect(p.name)[0] AS youngest, collect(p.name)[-2..] AS oldest").to_list()
// #{"oldest": ["josh", "peter"], "youngest": "vadas"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN p['na' || 'me'] AS name, {lang: 'java'}['lang'] AS lang").to_list()
// #{"lang": "java", "name": "marko"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'josh'}) RETURN p[$key] AS v", #{key: "age"}).to_list() // #{"v": 32}
g.gql("RETURN 'abc'[0] AS c") // error: cannot index a string The index of a list is an integer (x[1.0] and x['1'] are errors, GQLSTATUS 22G03; convert with toInteger(i)), the key of a map or an element a string; a text is no list (use substring(s, start, length)). These errors arise when a row reaches the expression, as for every other operand of the wrong kind ( Errors ).

385.body:
Three Cypher forms build maps and lists inside an expression. They are not GQL; gql() accepts them with their Neo4j meaning because Cypher users and LLMs write them all the time ( Cypher Aliases ). Together they give a JSON-shaped answer: one row per entity, its related data nested inside. v {.key, .*, x, key: expr} -- map projection of a vertex, an edge or a map
[x IN list] [x IN list WHERE cond] [x IN list | expr] [x IN list WHERE cond | expr]
[(a)-[:R]->(b) WHERE cond | expr] -- pattern comprehension, also [p = (a)-->(b) | p] Map projection. v {...} builds a map from the variable v: .key is its property (NULL when missing, the key kept), .* every stored property (no id, no labels), x the entry x: x, and key: expr any value, all in the written order. Its Gremlin twins are project() and value_map().by(unfold()); the GQL form is the record constructor {name: p.name}, which differs only for a NULL variable ( Semantics ): g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p {.name, .age, decade: p.age / 10} AS m ORDER BY p.name LIMIT 2").to_list()
// #{"m": #{"age": 32, "decade": 3, "name": "josh"}}, #{"m": #{"age": 29, "decade": 2, "name": "marko"}}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN p {.*} AS m").to_list() // #{"m": #{"age": 29, "name": "marko"}}
g.v().has("name", "marko").value_map().by(__.unfold()).to_list() // #{"age": 29, "name": "marko"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'})-[e:knows]->(f) RETURN e {.*, to: f.name} AS k ORDER BY k.to").to_list()
// #{"k": #{"to": "josh", "weight": 1.0}}, #{"k": #{"to": "vadas", "weight": 0.5}} List comprehension. [x IN list WHERE cond | expr] walks the list in its order, keeps the items for which cond is TRUE and maps each kept item to expr; leave out WHERE to keep every item, | expr to keep the item itself. x is a new name that exists only inside the brackets. The Gremlin twin is local(__.unfold().is(..).fold()): g.gql("RETURN [x IN range(1, 5) WHERE x % 2 = 1] AS odd, [x IN range(1, 3) | x * x] AS sq, [x IN [1, 2] | [y IN [10, 20] | x * y]] AS grid").to_list()
// #{"grid": [[10, 20], [20, 40]], "odd": [1, 3, 5], "sq": [1, 4, 9]}
g.inject([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]).local(__.unfold().is(P.gt(2)).fold()).to_list() // [3, 4, 5]
g.gql("MATCH p = (a {name: 'marko'})-[:knows|created]->{2}(b) RETURN [x IN nodes(p) | x.name] AS names ORDER BY b.name").to_list()
// #{"names": ["marko", "josh", "lop"]}, #{"names": ["marko", "josh", "ripple"]}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS name, [x IN [p.age, p.age + 10] WHERE x > 30] AS l ORDER BY name LIMIT 2").to_list()
// #{"l": [32, 42], "name": "josh"}, #{"l": [39], "name": "marko"} A | inside the list or the WHERE part ends that part, so a label alternative there needs parentheses: [x IN l WHERE x:(A|B) | x.name]. Pattern comprehension. [(a)-[:R]->(b) WHERE cond | expr] is the list of expr for every match of the pattern on the row: [] when nothing matches, never NULL. The pattern has at least one edge; it reads the variables bound before it (they are imported, like in EXISTS ), and the names it binds itself (b here, or a path variable p = ...) exist only inside the brackets. It is a subquery: the Gremlin twin is project(..).by(__.out("knows").values("name").fold()), and the items come in no defined order. g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN [(p)-[:knows]->(f) | f.name] AS friends").to_list() // #{"friends": ["josh", "vadas"]}, in any order
g.v().has("name", "marko").project("friends").by(__.out("knows").values("name").fold()).to_list() // #{"friends": ["josh", "vadas"]}
g.gql("MATCH (s:software {name: 'ripple'}) RETURN [(s)<-[:created]-(p) WHERE p.age > 30 | p.name] AS older").to_list() // #{"older": ["josh"]}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE size([(p)-[:created]->(s) | s]) >= 2 RETURN p.name AS name").to_list() // #{"name": "josh"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN [q = (p)-[:knows]->() | path_length(q)] AS l").to_list() // #{"l": [1, 1]} Together, one row per person with its friends and projects nested inside, the shape a JSON API or an LLM wants ( Result Shape ): g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p {.name, friends: [(p)-[:knows]->(f) | f.name], projects: [(p)-[:created]->(s) | s {.name, .lang}]} AS person ORDER BY person.name").to_json()
// [{"person": {"name": "josh", "friends": [], "projects": [{"name": "ripple", "lang": "java"}, ...]}}, ...] All three stand anywhere an expression does (WHERE, RETURN, WITH, LET, FILTER, FOR, ORDER BY, an aggregate's argument) and nest in each other. Their rules: A comprehension variable must be a new name : one that is in scope, or that the query uses anywhere else, is an error ( Semantics ). No aggregate inside the WHERE or | part (aggregate in a WITH first, or aggregate the list: size([..])). A list-comprehension variable cannot be read inside an EXISTS or a pattern comprehension in its WHERE or | part yet ( Not supported yet ). A pattern comprehension counts as a subquery: a query holds at most 64 subqueries ( Errors ). The list predicates all(), any(), none(), single() and reduce() follow the same rules for their variables (below). g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN [p IN [1, 2] | p * 2] AS l") // error: the comprehension variable `p` shadows the variable `p` in scope
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN [(p)-[:knows]->(f) | count(f)] AS l") // error: count() cannot be used inside the | part of a pattern comprehension
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS n, [(p)-[:knows]->(f) | f.name] AS l ORDER BY f.name") // error: `f` is bound inside a pattern comprehension
g.gql("RETURN size([x IN [1, 2, 3] WHERE x > 0]) = size([1, 2, 3]) AS all_positive").to_list() // #{"all_positive": true}

