The temporal values are the engine's five date and time types (date, time, localdatetime, datetime, duration): a value gql() builds is the same value a Gremlin step or a stored property holds, and a stored date reads as a date (n.born.year). The functions are openCypher's (Neo4j Cypher 25) with Neo4j's meaning; the constructors also have their ISO GQL names. The places where an answer differs from Neo4j are on Semantics , and the Gremlin step that does the same as each function on Dates in GQL . ISO GQL Cypher Gives (engine type) date(x) date(x) a calendar day (date) local_time(x) localtime(x) a time of day without an offset (time) local_datetime(x) localdatetime(x) a date and a time of day without an offset (localdatetime) zoned_datetime(x) datetime(x) an instant with its UTC offset (datetime) duration(x) duration(x) months, days and a time part (duration) x is ISO 8601 text, a map of fields, or (not for duration) another temporal value, which is projected onto the type (date(datetime(..)) is the date at the value's own offset, datetime(localdatetime(..)) the wall clock at UTC). With no argument a constructor gives the time the query started ( Clocks ). A NULL argument, or a NULL field of the map, gives NULL. Values hold microseconds : text or a field with a non-zero part below the microsecond is 0A000 "not yet", never cut ( Errors ). g.gql("RETURN date('2015-07-21') AS d, localtime('21:40:32.142') AS t, localdatetime('2015-07-21T21:40') AS ldt, datetime('2015-07-21T21:40+01:00') AS dt").to_list()
// #{"d": date("2015-07-21"), "dt": datetime("2015-07-21T21:40:00+01:00"), "ldt": localdatetime("2015-07-21T21:40:00"), "t": time("21:40:32.142")}
g.gql("RETURN local_time('10:00') AS t, local_datetime('2015-07-21T21:40') AS ldt, zoned_datetime('2015-07-21T21:40:32+02:00') AS dt").to_list()
// #{"dt": datetime("2015-07-21T21:40:32+02:00"), "ldt": localdatetime("2015-07-21T21:40:00"), "t": time("10:00:00")}
g.gql("RETURN date(datetime('2015-07-21T23:40:32+02:00')) AS d, localtime(datetime('2015-07-21T23:40:32+02:00')) AS t, datetime(localdatetime('2015-07-21T12:00')) AS dt").to_list()
// #{"d": date("2015-07-21"), "dt": datetime("2015-07-21T12:00:00Z"), "t": time("23:40:32")}
g.gql("RETURN date(null) AS a, date({year: 2015, month: null}) AS b").to_list() // #{"a": (), "b": ()} Text. The constructors read ISO 8601 as Neo4j does, in the extended and the basic form: Type Text forms (examples) date 2015-07-21 / 20150721, 2015-07 / 201507, 2015; week dates 2015-W30-2 / 2015W302, 2015-W30 (its Monday); ordinal dates 2015-202 / 2015202; quarter dates 2015-Q3-21 / 2015Q321, 2015-Q3 localtime 21:40:32.142 / 214032.142, 21:40 / 2140, 21; a comma for the fraction (21:40:32,142); no offset (12:00+01:00 is 22007) localdatetime a date form, T, a time form: 2015-07-21T21:40, 2015-W30-2T2140, 2015202T21:40:32.142 datetime a local datetime with Z, +01:00, +0100 or +01, and / or a zone in brackets: 2015-07-21T21:40:32.142[Europe/London], ..+01:00[Europe/London] (the offset must be the zone's at that time); a date alone is midnight; no offset and no zone is UTC duration P14DT16H12M, P5W, -P1D, P-1DT2H; a fraction on any unit (P1.5Y, PT0.75M, PT1.5H); the alternative form P0001-02-03T04:05:06 g.gql("RETURN date('2015W302') AS w, date('2015-202') AS o, date('2015-Q3-21') AS q, date('2015-W30') AS monday").to_list()
// #{"monday": date("2015-07-20"), "o": date("2015-07-21"), "q": date("2015-07-21"), "w": date("2015-07-21")}
g.gql("RETURN datetime('2015-07-21T21:40:32.142[Europe/London]') AS a, datetime('2015-07-21T21:40:32+0100') AS b, datetime('2015-07-21') AS c").to_list()
// #{"a": datetime("2015-07-21T21:40:32.142+01:00"), "b": datetime("2015-07-21T21:40:32+01:00"), "c": datetime("2015-07-21T00:00:00Z")}
g.gql("RETURN duration('P1Y2M10DT2H30M') AS a, duration('P1.5Y') AS b, duration('PT0.75M') AS c, duration('P0001-02-03T04:05:06') AS d").to_list()
// #{"a": duration("P1Y2M10DT2H30M"), "b": duration("P1Y6M"), "c": duration("PT45S"), "d": duration("P1Y2M3DT4H5M6S")}
g.gql("RETURN date('2015-13-01') AS d") // fails: the date does not exist (22007) Maps. A map names the fields; the keys match in any ASCII case: Type Keys date year with one group: month / day; week / dayOfWeek (ISO 8601 weeks: year is then the week-numbering year); ordinalDay; quarter / dayOfQuarter. A missing smaller field is its first value ({year: 1984} is January 1) localtime hour, minute, second, and millisecond, microsecond, nanosecond (added up: {millisecond: 645, microsecond: 876} is .645876) localdatetime the keys of both datetime the keys of both and timezone ('+01:00', 'Europe/Stockholm'); no timezone is UTC base values date: d takes the date (of a date, a local datetime or a datetime at its own offset), time: t the time of day (and the offset of a datetime t), datetime: x both; the other keys then override single fields ({date: d, day: 28}, {date: d, week: 1} keeps the day of the week). With a base that has an offset, timezone converts the instant (12:00+01:00 to 16:00+05:00); without one it attaches the zone duration years, months, weeks, days, hours, minutes, seconds, milliseconds, microseconds, nanoseconds, each may be fractional and negative; a fraction cascades down (a year is 12 months, a month 2 629 746 seconds, a week 7 days, a day 86 400 seconds, then the whole days of the seconds become days: {months: 0.75} is P22DT19H51M49.5S) An unknown key, or two groups of date keys together, is 22007; a field out of range (month: 13, day: 30 in February, hour: 25, the year 10000) is 22008. g.gql("RETURN date({year: 2015, week: 30, dayOfWeek: 2}) AS w, date({year: 1984, quarter: 3, dayOfQuarter: 45}) AS q, localdatetime({year: 1984, ordinalDay: 202, hour: 12}) AS o").to_list()
// #{"o": localdatetime("1984-07-20T12:00:00"), "q": date("1984-08-14"), "w": date("2015-07-21")}
g.gql("RETURN localtime({hour: 12, minute: 31, second: 14, millisecond: 645, microsecond: 876}) AS t, datetime({year: 1984, month: 10, day: 11, hour: 12, timezone: 'Europe/Stockholm'}) AS dt").to_list()
// #{"dt": datetime("1984-10-11T12:00:00+01:00"), "t": time("12:31:14.645876")}
g.gql("RETURN date({date: date('2015-07-21'), day: 28}) AS a, localdatetime({date: date('2015-07-21'), time: localtime('12:30')}) AS b, datetime({datetime: datetime('2015-07-21T12:00+01:00'), timezone: '+05:00'}) AS c").to_list()
// #{"a": date("2015-07-28"), "b": localdatetime("2015-07-21T12:30:00"), "c": datetime("2015-07-21T16:00:00+05:00")}
g.gql("RETURN duration({days: 14, hours: 16, minutes: 12}) AS a, duration({years: 1.5, weeks: 2, seconds: 1.25}) AS b, duration({months: 0.75}) AS c").to_list()
// #{"a": duration("P14DT16H12M"), "b": duration("P1Y6M14DT1.250S"), "c": duration("P22DT19H51M49.500S")}
g.gql("RETURN date({year: 2015, month: 2, day: 30}) AS d") // fails: the field day cannot be 30 (22008) datetime.fromepoch(seconds, nanoseconds) and datetime.fromepochmillis(milliseconds) give the datetime (UTC) of a time since 1970-01-01T00:00Z; the nanoseconds must be whole microseconds. The map form does the same as in Neo4j: datetime({epochSeconds: s}) with an optional millisecond / microsecond / nanosecond, or datetime({epochMillis: ms}), each with an optional timezone (the instant shown in that zone); no other field may stand next to them. g.gql("RETURN datetime.fromepoch(1416779, 999999000) AS a, datetime.fromepochmillis(237821673987) AS b").to_list()
// #{"a": datetime("1970-01-17T09:32:59.999999Z"), "b": datetime("1977-07-15T13:34:33.987Z")}
g.gql("RETURN datetime({epochSeconds: 1416779, microsecond: 5}) AS a, datetime({epochMillis: 237821673987, timezone: '+01:00'}) AS b").to_list()
// #{"a": datetime("1970-01-17T09:32:59.000005Z"), "b": datetime("1977-07-15T14:34:33.987+01:00")} Components x.name reads a component of a temporal value (any ASCII case: d.weekyear is d.weekYear). A name the value's type does not have is 22G03, and the message lists the names it has: Of Components date, localdatetime, datetime year, quarter (1-4), month, week and weekYear (ISO 8601: Monday weeks, week 1 holds January 4), dayOfQuarter (also quarterDay), day, ordinalDay (1-366), dayOfWeek (also weekDay; 1 = Monday to 7 = Sunday) localtime, localdatetime, datetime hour, minute, second, millisecond, microsecond, nanosecond, each fraction counted from the start of the second (.645876 gives 645, 645876, 645876000) datetime timezone and offset (the offset text: Z, +01:00; never a zone name), offsetMinutes, offsetSeconds, epochSeconds, epochMillis duration the totals years, quarters, months, weeks, days, hours, minutes, seconds, milliseconds, microseconds, nanoseconds; the remainders quartersOfYear, monthsOfQuarter, monthsOfYear, daysOfWeek, minutesOfHour, secondsOfMinute, millisecondsOfSecond, microsecondsOfSecond, nanosecondsOfSecond A duration's months, days and seconds stay apart: years and quarters count its months, weeks its days, hours to nanoseconds its time part (duration('P1M2D').days is 2). The divisions truncate toward zero (duration({months: -16}).years is -1). g.gql("RETURN date('2017-10-11').year AS y, date('2017-10-11').week AS w, date('2017-10-11').dayOfWeek AS dw, date('1984-01-01').weekYear AS wy, date('1984-10-11').ordinalDay AS o").to_list()
// #{"dw": 3, "o": 285, "w": 41, "wy": 1983, "y": 2017}
g.gql("RETURN datetime('2015-07-21T21:40:32.142+01:00').epochMillis AS e, datetime('2015-07-21T21:40:32.142+01:00').offset AS o, localtime('12:31:14.645876').millisecond AS ms").to_list()
// #{"e": 1437511232142, "ms": 645, "o": "+01:00"}
g.gql("RETURN duration({months: 14, days: 3, seconds: 3725}).years AS y, duration({months: 14, days: 3, seconds: 3725}).monthsOfYear AS my, duration({months: 14, days: 3, seconds: 3725}).hours AS h").to_list()
// #{"h": 1, "my": 2, "y": 1}
g.gql("RETURN date('2015-01-01').hour AS h") // fails: a date has no component hour (22G03) Arithmetic and comparison Form Result x + dur, dur + x, x - dur (x a date, localtime, localdatetime or datetime) a value of x's type: the duration's months (the day clamped to the month's end), then its days, then its time part, each where the type has it dur + dur, dur - dur part by part (P1D + PT49H is P1DT49H) dur * n, n * dur, dur / n each part scaled, with the fraction cascade of duration({..}) (P1M * 0.5 is P15DT5H14M33S) -dur every part negated A date takes the months, the days and the whole days of the time part (toward zero: PT33H adds one day, PT-33H takes one away), a localtime only the time part, wrapping around midnight. A datetime keeps its offset. Two values that are not both durations cannot be added (22G03: the time between them is duration.between(a, b)); dur / 0 is 22012; a result outside the years -9999 to 9999 is 22008. g.gql("RETURN date('2024-01-31') + duration('P1M') AS a, date('1997-09-11') + duration('P29DT33H') AS b, localtime('23:00') + duration('PT2H') AS c, localdatetime('2015-07-21T21:40') - duration('PT1H30M') AS d").to_list()
// #{"a": date("2024-02-29"), "b": date("1997-10-11"), "c": time("01:00:00"), "d": localdatetime("2015-07-21T20:10:00")}
g.gql("RETURN duration('P1M') * 0.5 AS a, duration('P1Y') / 2 AS b, duration('P1D') + duration('PT49H') AS c, -duration('P1D') AS d").to_list()
// #{"a": duration("P15DT5H14M33S"), "b": duration("P6M"), "c": duration("P1DT49H"), "d": duration("-P1D")}
g.gql("RETURN date('2015-01-01') + date('2015-01-01') AS x") // fails: + adds a duration to a temporal value (22G03) Values of one type compare by calendar and clock, two datetimes by their instant (12:00+01:00 = 11:00Z is TRUE). Two durations are equal when their months, days and time part are, but they are not ordered : <, >, <=, >= on two durations are NULL (a month has no fixed length); ORDER BY still sorts them, by their length with a month of 30 days. Values of different temporal types are never equal (= FALSE) and not ordered (NULL), as values of different kinds are ( Comparisons across types ). g.gql("RETURN datetime('2015-07-21T12:00+01:00') = datetime('2015-07-21T11:00Z') AS a, date('2015-01-01') < date('2016-01-01') AS b, duration('P1M') < duration('P30DT1S') AS c, date('2015-01-01') = localdatetime('2015-01-01T00:00') AS d").to_list()
// #{"a": true, "b": true, "c": (), "d": false}
g.gql("FOR d IN [duration('P1M'), duration('P30DT1S'), duration('PT1H')] RETURN d ORDER BY d").to_list()
// #{"d": duration("PT1H")}, #{"d": duration("P1M")}, #{"d": duration("P30DT1S")} toString(x) writes a temporal value in Neo4j's text ('12:00', '1984-10-11T12:31', 'PT-1M-0.001S': no zero seconds, trailing fraction zeros stripped, a sign per duration component), while CAST(x AS STRING) writes the engine's canonical ISO 8601 text ('12:00:00', '-PT1M0.001S', Text Formats ). The constructors read both back. g.gql("RETURN toString(localtime('12:00')) AS a, CAST(localtime('12:00') AS STRING) AS b, toString(duration({minutes: -1, milliseconds: -1})) AS c, CAST(duration({minutes: -1, milliseconds: -1}) AS STRING) AS d").to_list()
// #{"a": "12:00", "b": "12:00:00", "c": "PT-1M-0.001S", "d": "-PT1M0.001S"} Truncation date.truncate(unit, x [, map]), localtime.truncate(..), localdatetime.truncate(..) and datetime.truncate(..) cut x to the start of unit and give a value of their type (these qualified names are Cypher's; GQL has no spelling for them): Function Units (any ASCII case) date.truncate millennium, century, decade, year, weekYear, quarter, month, week, day localdatetime.truncate, datetime.truncate those, and hour, minute, second, millisecond, microsecond, nanosecond localtime.truncate day (midnight) to nanosecond millennium and century follow Neo4j: 2017 truncates to 2000, 1984 to 1900 (Gremlin's date_trunc keeps PostgreSQL's 2001 and 1901). weekYear is the Monday of ISO week 1 of the week-numbering year (1984-01-01 gives 1983-01-03); week is a Monday; nanosecond keeps the value (it holds microseconds). x may be of another temporal type and is projected first: the date of a datetime at its own offset, the time of day of either, midnight for a date. datetime.truncate keeps the offset of a datetime and gives UTC to a value without one. The map overrides fields of the truncated value as the constructors' base form does: {day: 2}, {dayOfWeek: 2}, {hour: 1}, {millisecond: 5}, and for datetime.truncate {timezone: ..}, which keeps the wall clock and reads it in that zone. A unit the type lacks (date.truncate('hour', ..)) or an unknown unit is 22023; a value without the part the type needs (date.truncate('day', localtime(..))) or a duration 22G03. g.gql("RETURN date.truncate('millennium', date('2017-10-11')) AS m, date.truncate('century', date('1984-10-11')) AS c, date.truncate('weekYear', date('1984-01-01')) AS wy").to_list()
// #{"c": date("1900-01-01"), "m": date("2000-01-01"), "wy": date("1983-01-03")}
g.gql("RETURN date.truncate('week', date('2017-10-11'), {dayOfWeek: 2}) AS a, localdatetime.truncate('quarter', localdatetime('2017-11-11T12:31:14'), {day: 2}) AS b, localtime.truncate('hour', localtime('12:31:14'), {minute: 5}) AS c").to_list()
// #{"a": date("2017-10-10"), "b": localdatetime("2017-10-02T00:00:00"), "c": time("12:05:00")}
g.gql("RETURN datetime.truncate('month', date('2017-11-11')) AS a, datetime.truncate('day', datetime('2017-10-11T12:31:14+01:00'), {timezone: 'Europe/Stockholm'}) AS b").to_list()
// #{"a": datetime("2017-11-01T00:00:00Z"), "b": datetime("2017-10-11T00:00:00+02:00")}
g.gql("RETURN date.truncate('hour', date('2017-10-11')) AS d") // fails: the unit hour is not defined for a date (22023) Durations between two values Function Gives duration.between(a, b) the whole months from a to b (a month counts once its day of the month and time of day are reached), then the whole days after them, then the rest of the time; every part has the sign of the direction duration.inMonths(a, b) the whole months only (toward zero) duration.inDays(a, b) the whole days only (toward zero) duration.inSeconds(a, b) the elapsed time, as a duration of seconds only The operands may be of different types; they are aligned first as Neo4j aligns them: when only one has a time of day, the other gets midnight; when only one has a date, the other gets that date (date('1984-10-11') to localtime('16:30') is PT16H30M); when only one has an offset, the other is read at that offset. inMonths / inDays of two localtimes, a duration or a value that is no temporal value is 22G03. g.gql("RETURN duration.between(date('1984-10-11'), date('2015-06-24')) AS d, duration.inMonths(date('1984-10-11'), date('2015-06-24')) AS m, duration.inDays(date('1984-10-11'), date('2015-06-24')) AS dd, duration.inSeconds(date('1984-10-11'), date('1984-10-12')) AS s").to_list()
// #{"d": duration("P30Y8M13D"), "dd": duration("P11213D"), "m": duration("P30Y8M"), "s": duration("PT24H")}
g.gql("RETURN duration.between(localdatetime('2015-07-21T21:40:32.142'), date('2015-06-24')) AS a, duration.between(date('1984-10-11'), localtime('16:30')) AS b").to_list()
// #{"a": duration("-P27DT21H40M32.142S"), "b": duration("PT16H30M")}
g.gql("RETURN duration.inDays(localtime('10:00'), localtime('12:00')) AS d") // fails: two local times have no days (22G03) Clocks date(), localtime(), localdatetime() and datetime() with no argument (and their ISO names), and X.statement() / X.transaction() (date.statement(), datetime.transaction(), ...) give the time the query started: one instant per query , the same for every row and every call, so a query is deterministic and repeatable. A zone argument or map gives that instant in the zone (datetime.statement('Europe/Berlin'), date({timezone: '+05:00'})); the default zone is UTC. In a script the instant is the script's own: datetime() in g.gql(..) equals the script's now(), and date() its today() at UTC. X.realtime(), a clock that moves while the query runs, is refused like rand(). g.gql("RETURN date() = date.statement() AS a, datetime.transaction() = datetime() AS b, datetime({timezone: '+05:00'}).timezone AS z").to_list() // #{"a": true, "b": true, "z": "+05:00"}
g.gql("FOR i IN [1, 2, 3] RETURN count(DISTINCT datetime()) AS instants").to_list() // #{"instants": 1}
g.gql("RETURN datetime.realtime() AS t") // refused: a clock that moves while the query runs Not yet Cypher's zoned time() (and time.truncate, time.statement, ...) and ISO GQL's zoned_time(): a time of day with an offset has no engine type yet; use localtime() or a datetime(). A temporal value below the microsecond; timestamp(), current_date() and the other date functions named under Not supported yet ; the GQL literals DATE '..', DURATION '..' and CAST(x AS DATE). Each is 0A000 with the alternative. g.gql("RETURN time('12:00+01:00') AS t") // error: time() (a time of day with a UTC offset) is not yet supported
g.gql("RETURN localtime('12:31:14.645876123') AS t") // error: a temporal value with a part below the microsecond is not yet supported

