$name reads the value of name from the parameter map, the second argument of gql(). A parameter is a value, never query text: it cannot change the query, whatever it holds. A parameter the query reads but the map lacks is an error before the run; extra entries are ignored. g.gql("MATCH (n:person) WHERE n.age > $min RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name", #{min: 30}).to_list() // #{"name": "josh"}, #{"name": "peter"}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) WHERE n.name IN $names RETURN n.name AS name, n.age AS age ORDER BY name", #{names: ["josh", "vadas"]}).to_list()
// #{"age": 32, "name": "josh"}, #{"age": 27, "name": "vadas"}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person {name: $who})-[:created]->(s) RETURN s.name AS s", #{who: "peter"}).to_list() // #{"s": "lop"}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name SKIP $s LIMIT $l", #{s: 2, l: 5}).to_list() // #{"name": "peter"}, #{"name": "vadas"} A parameter may also stand where an index can serve it, so MATCH (p:person {name: $n}) uses a property index on person.name when one exists ( Plans and Performance ).

