Within one MATCH clause an edge is bound at most once (GQL's DIFFERENT EDGES match mode, also called trail semantics; the GQL default and Neo4j's). Vertices may repeat. g.gql("MATCH (a)-[:created]->(s)<-[:created]-(b) WHERE s.name = 'lop' RETURN a.name AS a, b.name AS b ORDER BY a, b").to_list()
// josh/marko, josh/peter, marko/josh, marko/peter, peter/josh, peter/marko: never a person with themselves
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[:knows]->(b)<-[:knows]-(c) RETURN c.name AS c").to_list() // no rows: c could only be marko again over the same edge The second query has no rows: every knows edge into b comes from marko, and binding it again for c would use the same edge twice. Gremlin has no such rule, so its twin returns marko: g.v().has("name", "marko").out("knows").in("knows").values("name").to_list() // "marko", "marko" Two separate MATCH clauses are checked separately: an edge bound in the first may be bound again in the second. An edge variable written twice in one MATCH is an error. g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[:knows]->(b) MATCH (b)<-[:knows]-(c) RETURN c.name AS c").to_list() // #{"c": "marko"}, #{"c": "marko"}
g.gql("MATCH (a)-[e]->(b), (b)<-[e]-(a) RETURN a") // error: the edge variable `e` is used for more than one edge in the same MATCH

