A quantified path pattern ((a) ((x)-[e]->(y)){1,3} (b), The Supported Subset ) repeats a whole chain. Graphersal follows ISO GQL and, where a detail is open, Neo4j's Cypher 25, which implements the same construct: Merge rule. The node before the group is the first node of the first iteration, the node after it the last node of the last iteration, and each iteration starts where the previous one ended: in (a) ((x)-->(y)){1,3} (b), a is the first x, b the last y, and the y of one iteration is the x of the next. After zero iterations a and b are one node, so (a) ((x)-->(y))* (a) matches the closed walks from a (the zero-length one included). Trail per body edge. Within one match every edge is different: across the iterations, between the body's edges and from the other edges of the MATCH clause, so a quantified path pattern ends on every finite graph, like a quantified edge. A body edge that a ? part did not bind (zero iterations) clashes with nothing. Group lists. With a quantifier other than ?, each body variable is the list of its elements, one per iteration, in the order the text reads (also when the plan walks the pattern from its far end): x = [x1, x2, ...]. A zero-length match gives []. A property of a group variable has no single value outside the body (42002); inside the body, in its conditions, the variable is the element of the current iteration. ? gives nullable singletons (ISO). ((x)-[e]->(y))? matches its body once or not at all; its variables are single elements, NULL for the match where it did not match (no iteration), not lists. {0,1} matches the same paths but keeps group lists ([] or one element). No quantifier: singletons. A parenthesized path pattern without a quantifier matches its body exactly once; its variables are single elements, as if the parentheses were not there, and its WHERE is a condition on that part of the path. The body condition runs in every iteration and reads only body variables, parameters and values: an iteration whose condition is not TRUE ends that walk. g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[e]->(m) ((x)-[f]-(y))? (b) RETURN m.name AS m, b.name AS b ORDER BY m, b").to_list()
// josh/josh, josh/lop, josh/ripple, lop/josh, lop/lop, lop/peter, vadas/vadas: never back to marko over the edge e
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'vadas'}) ((x)-[:created]->(s))? (q) RETURN s AS s, q.name AS q").to_list() // #{"q": "vadas", "s": ()}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'vadas'}) ((x)-[:created]->(s)){0,1} (q) RETURN s AS s, q.name AS q").to_list() // #{"q": "vadas", "s": []} Limits that hold now (E17; each refused with 0A000 and a help line naming the rewrite): one chain per body, no quantifier nested inside a body (neither a group nor a quantified edge), no path variable inside a body (name the whole path instead), and a body variable is a new name: an outer variable written inside a body, a variable written twice in one body, a body condition reading an outer variable, and a subquery inside a body are not supported yet. Every body edge counts once toward the 64 edge patterns of a MATCH clause, however often the body repeats. The unbounded quantifiers ({m,}, *, +) are bounded as for a quantified edge: repeat.max_loops counts the iterations of the whole body, traversal.max_traversers the live rows, and reaching either is an error naming the pattern ( Errors ), never a shortened result. Reading ((. Where an expression stands, (( followed by a pattern and a closing ) with {..}, ?, a node, an edge, or * / + not followed by an operand reads as a quantified path pattern (a pattern predicate). So ((a)-->(b)) + (c) is the pattern ((a)-->(b))+ (c), not the boolean of the pattern predicate ((a)-->(b)) plus c; with an operand after it (((a)-->(b)) + [1], + x, + 1) it stays arithmetic. Arithmetic on a predicate's boolean is a type error (22G03) in either reading, so no working query changes meaning.

