for d in [ ["Cats and dogs", [0.9, 0.1, 0.0]], ["Training a puppy", [0.8, 0.1, 0.2]], ["Feeding your cat", [0.6, 0.7, 0.0]], ["Pasta at home", [0.0, 0.9, 0.1]], ["Robot chefs", [0.1, 0.6, 0.8]], ["Rust for beginners", [0.0, 0.0, 1.0]], ["GPS collars for dogs", [0.7, 0.0, 0.6]],
] { g.add_v("doc").property("title", d[0]).property("emb", d[1]).iterate();
} // Like "Feeding your cat" (animals and food): ["Pasta at home", "Cats and dogs"]
g.v().has("title", "Feeding your cat").as("me").values("emb").as("q") .v().has_label("doc").where(P.neq("me")) .nearest("emb", __.select("q"), 2) .values("title").to_list()

400.body:
A property that is missing reads as NULL, exactly like a property stored as null. WHERE uses three-valued logic: a comparison with NULL is NULL (unknown), AND / OR / NOT follow the SQL / GQL truth tables, and a row passes only when the condition is TRUE. g.gql("MATCH (n) RETURN n.name AS name, n.age > 30 AS old, n.lang = 'java' AS java ORDER BY name").to_list()
// josh: old true, java (); lop: old (), java true; marko: old false, java (); ...
g.gql("MATCH (n) RETURN n.age = NULL AS a, NULL IS NULL AS b, 2 IN [1, NULL] AS c, NULL IN [] AS d LIMIT 1").to_list()
// #{"a": (), "b": true, "c": (), "d": false}
g.gql("MATCH (n) WHERE n.age IS NULL RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // #{"name": "lop"}, #{"name": "ripple"} (() is how Rhai shows NULL; JSON shows null.) Test for a missing value with IS NULL, never with = NULL. The consequence that surprises Gremlin users: NOT of an unknown condition is still unknown, so the software (which has no age) passes neither n.age < 30 nor NOT n.age < 30, while Gremlin's not(has(..)) keeps them: g.gql("MATCH (n) WHERE NOT n.age < 30 RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // #{"name": "josh"}, #{"name": "peter"}
g.v().not(__.has("age", P.lt(30))).values("name").order().to_list() // "josh", "lop", "peter", "ripple"
g.gql("MATCH (n) WHERE n.age <> 29 RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // josh, peter, vadas (not lop, not ripple) The same holds for <>: a vertex without age is not "different from 29", its comparison is unknown.

401.body:
A row is kept once, however often the body matches. EXISTS (and a pattern predicate, The Supported Subset ) is a semi-join: it asks whether there is a match and adds no rows. The same pattern in MATCH gives one row per match, so josh, who created two programs, comes twice: g.gql("MATCH (p:person)-[:created]->() RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // josh, josh, marko, peter
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE (p)-[:created]->() RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // josh, marko, peter TRUE or FALSE, never NULL. A body that matches is TRUE, one that does not is FALSE, also when it reads a variable that is NULL on the row (an import from an OPTIONAL MATCH that did not match): a NULL element matches nothing, so EXISTS is FALSE and NOT EXISTS TRUE. Around it, three-valued logic works as usual: NULL OR EXISTS {..} is TRUE when the body matches and NULL otherwise. (This follows Neo4j; the ISO text is still to be checked.) g.gql("MATCH (p:person) OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s WHERE s.name = 'ripple') RETURN p.name AS name, EXISTS { (s)<-[:created]-() } AS made, NOT EXISTS { (s)<-[:created]-() } AS not_made ORDER BY name").to_list()
// josh: made true, not_made false; marko, peter, vadas (s is NULL): made false, not_made true
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS name, p.lang = 'x' OR EXISTS { (p)-[:knows]->() } AS a ORDER BY name").to_list()
// josh (), marko true, peter (), vadas () A pattern alone is a condition, not a value. A pattern predicate such as (p)-[:knows]->() stands only where WHERE (or FILTER, a comprehension's WHERE, a WHEN of a searched CASE) expects a condition, also under NOT, AND, OR and XOR there. Anywhere else (a RETURN or WITH item, a function argument, a SET value) it is a syntax error (42001), as in Neo4j: write EXISTS { .. } for whether it matches, COUNT { .. } for how often, or a pattern comprehension for what it matches (stage 3f, the openCypher TCK Pattern1, List6): g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS name, (p)-[:knows]->() AS k") // error: the pattern stands where a value is expected (42001)
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS name, EXISTS { (p)-[:knows]->() } AS k, COUNT { (p)-[:knows]->() } AS n, [(p)-[:knows]->(f) | f.name] AS fs ORDER BY name").to_list()
// josh: false, 0, []; marko: true, 2, ["vadas", "josh"]; ... Trail per body clause. Each MATCH clause inside a body is its own clause for the trail rule ( Trail ): its edges are different from each other, but they may repeat an edge of the query outside the body (an imported edge variable is that same edge) or of another body clause. (Neo4j's rule; the ISO text is still to be checked.) g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'}) WHERE EXISTS { MATCH (a)-[:knows]->(x)<-[:knows]-(a) } RETURN a.name AS a").to_list() // no rows: one clause, one edge twice
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'}) WHERE EXISTS { MATCH (a)-[:knows]->(x) MATCH (x)<-[:knows]-(a) } RETURN a.name AS a").to_list() // #{"a": "marko"}
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[e:knows]->(b) WHERE EXISTS { MATCH (b)<-[e]-(x) } RETURN b.name AS b ORDER BY b").to_list() // josh, vadas: e imported Evaluated only when needed, at most once per row. A body stops at its first match. Where EXISTS stands inside a larger expression, a row runs the body only when the evaluation reaches it (a OR EXISTS {..} runs nothing when a is TRUE), and once even when several columns or keys of the row hold the same subquery. A body that imports nothing (EXISTS { (:robot) }) is still run per row; its answer is not cached yet. Filter order. The conditions of one element (or one WHERE) run in this order: those pushed into has(), the other plain conditions, then those holding a subquery, which cost the most (a subquery inside a larger expression before a plain [NOT] EXISTS). The result does not depend on the order, only the work does. The global optimizer rule filter_reorder may still move a subquery's step before a gql:filter by its own cost estimate, as it does for the same Gremlin steps ( Plans and Performance ).

402.body:
A NULL variable projects to NULL. A map projection v {..} of a NULL variable (an OPTIONAL MATCH that did not match) is NULL, and its entries are not evaluated at all, while the GQL record constructor {name: v.name} builds a map whose values are NULL. A pattern comprehension that imports a NULL variable matches nothing and gives [], never NULL: g.gql("MATCH (p:person) OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s {name: 'ripple'}) RETURN p.name AS name, s {.name} AS projection, {name: s.name} AS record, [(s)<-[:created]-(c) | c.name] AS creators ORDER BY name").to_list()
// josh: projection #{"name": "ripple"}, record #{"name": "ripple"}, creators ["josh"]
// marko, peter, vadas: projection (), record #{"name": ()}, creators []
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'vadas'}) OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) RETURN s {k: 1 / 0} AS m").to_list() // #{"m": ()}: the entry never runs Keys. The entries come in the written order. .* puts every stored property at its place, in stored order, without the id and the labels; a property stored as null is left out, as in properties(). A key written twice (also through .*) keeps its first place and takes the later value. The variable of a projection must be a vertex, an edge or a map; any other value is the data error 22G03. An element inside the map (friend: f) stays the live element ( Result Shape ). g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN p {.name, age: 1, .age} AS m").to_json() // [{"m": {"name": "marko", "age": 29}}]
g.gql("WITH {a: 1, b: 2} AS m RETURN m {.a, .c, .*, d: 3} AS x").to_json() // [{"x": {"a": 1, "c": null, "b": 2, "d": 3}}]
g.gql("WITH 1 AS m RETURN m {.a} AS x") // error: a map projection reads a node, an edge or a map, not a number (22G03) List comprehension. A NULL list gives NULL, a value that is not a list the data error 22G03. WHERE keeps an item only when the condition is TRUE: FALSE and NULL drop it ( three-valued logic ). The result keeps the list's order. g.gql("RETURN [x IN [1, NULL, 3] WHERE x > 1] AS kept, [x IN [1, NULL, 3] | x] AS every, [x IN NULL | x] AS none").to_list()
// #{"every": [1, (), 3], "kept": [3], "none": ()}
g.gql("RETURN [x IN 5 | x] AS m") // error: a list comprehension iterates over a list, not a number (22G03) List predicates and reduce. all(), any(), none() and single() are three-valued, not counts: a NULL list gives NULL; an item that decides the answer wins (all FALSE at a FALSE item, any TRUE and none FALSE at a TRUE one, single FALSE at a second TRUE one) and the later items are not evaluated; otherwise a NULL condition gives NULL. This is the openCypher TCK (Quantifier1 to Quantifier4), which Neo4j Cypher 25 follows; Neo4j's manual words the NULL rule of any() like that of all(), but its implementation and the TCK give any(x IN [NULL, 2] WHERE x = 2) TRUE, as here. reduce(acc = init, x IN l | expr) gives NULL for a NULL list, init for an empty one, and lets NULL items flow through expr. Their variables are new names like a comprehension's. g.gql("RETURN any(x IN [NULL, 2] WHERE x = 2) AS a, all(x IN [NULL, 2] WHERE x = 2) AS b, single(x IN [2, NULL] WHERE x = 2) AS c, single(x IN [2, NULL, 2] WHERE x = 2) AS d").to_list()
// #{"a": true, "b": (), "c": (), "d": false}
g.gql("RETURN reduce(s = 0, x IN [1, NULL] | s + x) AS a, reduce(s = 0, x IN [] | s + x) AS b, reduce(s = 0, x IN NULL | s + x) AS c").to_list()
// #{"a": (), "b": 0, "c": ()} A comprehension variable hides no name in scope. It may not shadow a variable or column in scope, nor the variable of a comprehension around it. A name that is no longer in scope (one a WITH dropped, one bound inside a subquery) may be used again, as in Neo4j (stage 3f, the openCypher TCK Quantifier1..4). One exception keeps a LET / WITH value right: a name that the expression of such a value in scope reads is refused too, since gql() substitutes the value by its expression, which still refers to the dropped variable by that name. So MATCH (n) WITH n AS m RETURN [n IN [1] | n], which runs in Neo4j, is an error here; pick another name ([x IN [1] | x]). The variable exists only inside its brackets. g.gql("MATCH (n:person) WITH n AS m RETURN [n IN [1] | n] AS l") // error: the comprehension variable `n` is a name that a LET or WITH value in scope still reads
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) WITH collect(n) AS ns RETURN [n IN ns | n.name] AS names").to_list() // #{"names": ["marko", "vadas", "josh", "peter"]}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) WITH n AS m RETURN [x IN [1] | x] AS l ORDER BY l LIMIT 1").to_list() // #{"l": [1]} Pattern comprehension: one item per match, in no defined order. The list holds one item per match of the pattern, so the same value may come several times (josh created two programs in java). Like any subquery it runs on the row's imports, with the trail rule inside its own pattern ( EXISTS ); its items come in the order the matches are found, which depends on the plan. When the order matters, match the pattern in the query, sort the rows and collect them with collect_list(), or sort the list after gql(). g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'josh'}) RETURN [(p)-[:created]->(s) | s.lang] AS langs").to_list() // #{"langs": ["java", "java"]}
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'}) RETURN [(a)-[:knows]->(x)<-[:knows]-(a) | x.name] AS one").to_list() // #{"one": []}: one pattern, one edge twice Evaluated when reached, once per row. A pattern comprehension runs its pattern only for the rows whose evaluation reaches it, at most once per row, like EXISTS; one that imports nothing still runs per row. Comprehensions nested from the same element multiply the work: [(m)-->(a) | [(m)-->(b) | ..]] runs the inner pattern once for every match of the outer one, like nested Gremlin map(..fold()) children, and only the resource limits bound a deep chain of them ( Resource limits ).

405.body:
Per row, joined. A CALL runs its body once for each incoming row, with the imported variables set to the row's values, and each row the body returns extends the incoming row: an incoming row with three results becomes three rows, one with none is dropped (OPTIONAL CALL keeps it once, the returned names NULL). A body that aggregates without grouping keys always returns one row (count 0, collect_list []), so it never drops a row. The body's DISTINCT, ORDER BY, OFFSET and LIMIT apply to one row's results: ORDER BY .. LIMIT k in a body is the top k per row, which a LIMIT of the whole query cannot express. g.gql("MATCH (p:person) CALL (p) { MATCH (p)-[:knows]->(f) RETURN f.name AS friend ORDER BY f.age DESC LIMIT 1 } RETURN p.name AS who, friend").to_list() // #{"friend": "josh", "who": "marko"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) OPTIONAL CALL (p) { MATCH (p)-[:knows]->(f) RETURN f.name AS friend ORDER BY f.age DESC LIMIT 1 } RETURN p.name AS who, friend ORDER BY who").to_list()
// josh (), marko josh, peter (), vadas () Imports. The body reads only what it imports: CALL (a, b) names them, CALL (*) takes every variable in scope, CALL { } and CALL () { } take none, and Cypher's importing WITH names them as the body's first clause ( Cypher Aliases ). A name the body binds that is also an outer name it did not import is a new variable of the body: MATCH (p) CALL { MATCH (p)-->(x) RETURN x } matches every p in the body, for every outer row. An imported NULL (from an OPTIONAL MATCH) matches nothing in the body's patterns, as in EXISTS. An uncorrelated body still runs per row. A body that imports nothing gives the same rows for every incoming row, but gql() does not cache them yet: it runs once for each row, so its cost multiplies with the rows before the CALL. Put such a CALL first, or compute the value once with a WITH before the MATCH that multiplies the rows: g.gql("MATCH (p:person) CALL { MATCH (s:software) RETURN count(s) AS n } RETURN p.name AS who, n ORDER BY who").to_list() // four rows with n 2: the count ran four times
g.gql("CALL { MATCH (s:software) RETURN count(s) AS n } MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS who, n ORDER BY who").to_list() // the same rows: the count ran once Not lazy inside one row. A CALL builds all the results of its body for one incoming row before the first of them moves on (Gremlin's flat_map()), so a LIMIT after the CALL stops the incoming rows, not a body run that has started: MATCH (p) CALL (p) { MATCH (p)-->{1,}(x) RETURN x } RETURN x LIMIT 1 still finds every x of the first p. Bound the body itself with its own LIMIT when only a few results per row are needed. The rows the body builds count against the resource limits like any other traversers. Labels in the body stay in the body. Variables the body binds and does not return are gone after it; reading one is an error, as for a subquery. Returned names must be new names.

