Targets. Each expression of a DELETE clause is evaluated per row and gives what the clause removes: a node, an edge, every node and edge of a path, or the items of a list (nested lists too). NULL (also inside a list) is skipped. A value of another kind is an error: before the run (42002) when the expression cannot give an element (a literal, arithmetic, a comparison), at run time (22G03) when only the row shows it (a WITH value, a list item). A label test (n:L, n IS L) is a syntax error (42001), since REMOVE n:L removes a label. One clause, all rows. The clause collects the targets of every row that reaches it, each element once (deleting the same element on several rows, or twice in one row, is no error, and an element an earlier clause removed is skipped), then removes the edges, then the nodes. NODETACH is checked per clause. Without DETACH, every node to remove must have no edges except those the same clause removes; the check covers the whole clause before anything is removed, so a failing check leaves the graph as it was and fails the query (G1001). DETACH DELETE removes the edges of each node first. g.gql("MATCH (a)-[r]-(b) DELETE r, a, b RETURN count(*) AS n").to_list() // #{"n": 12}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person {name: 'marko'}) DELETE n") // error: G1001, the node still has edges Reading a deleted element is an error. After the clause, a property, labels(), element_id(), keys(), properties(), an endpoint or a sort key of a deleted element fails the query (the root cause is the storage's "element removed"), never a silent NULL or empty value. Returning the element itself fails too (RETURN n, a path holding it, also when a LIMIT 0 follows, since the RETURN builds each row before the LIMIT counts it). Three reads still work: count(n), n IS NULL (false: the variable holds a deleted element), and type(e) of a deleted edge, which gives the label it had (the openCypher tests expect it). A pattern that walks from a deleted node finds no edges. Values computed before the DELETE keep their value: a WITH n, n.age AS age before it is evaluated before the clause runs. g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'vadas'}) WITH p, p.age AS age DETACH DELETE p RETURN age, p IS NULL AS gone").to_list() // #{"age": 27, "gone": false}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'vadas'}) DETACH DELETE p RETURN labels(p) AS l") // error: the node was deleted earlier in this query A deliberate deviation from Neo4j: Neo4j returns a node or relationship deleted earlier in the same query from RETURN n; gql() fails the query with a help naming the properties(n) pattern (see the table in Writes ). An error can be relaxed later without changing a query that works.

