Numbers compare by value across integers and floats (1 = 1.0 is TRUE). Strings, booleans, UUIDs and dates compare within their own kind. Comparing values of different kinds is never an error and never a conversion: = is FALSE and <> TRUE (a number is not equal to a string), while the orderings <, <=, >, >= are NULL (a number is neither smaller nor larger than a string). A NULL operand gives NULL for every operator: g.gql("MATCH (n) RETURN n.name AS n, n.age > 'a' AS cmp ORDER BY n LIMIT 2").to_list() // #{"cmp": (), "n": "josh"}, #{"cmp": (), "n": "lop"}
g.gql("RETURN 1 = '1' AS a, [1] <> 1 AS b, 1 IN ['1'] AS c, 1 < 'a' AS d").to_list() // #{"a": false, "b": true, "c": false, "d": ()} = and <> also compare lists (item by item, three-valued: [1, 2] = [null, 'foo'] is FALSE because 2 and 'foo' differ, [1, 2] = [null, 2] is NULL), maps (key by key) and elements (the same vertex or edge). Lists are also ordered , lexicographically: the first pair of items that is not equal decides, a NULL (or incomparable) pair reached first makes the result NULL, and a list that is a prefix of the other is the smaller one: g.gql("RETURN [1, 2] < [1, 3] AS a, [1, null] >= [1] AS b, [1, 2] >= [1, null] AS c, [1, 2] >= [3, null] AS d").to_list() // #{"a": true, "b": true, "c": (), "d": false} Maps, elements and paths are not ordered (NULL); ORDER BY still sorts them ( Ordering ). Floats follow IEEE 754. A float divided by zero is not an error: 1.0 / 0.0 is Infinity, -1 / 0.0 is -Infinity, 0.0 / 0.0 and 1.0 % 0.0 are NaN; an integer divided by zero stays error 22012, and a float result that overflows from finite operands (1.0e308 * 10) stays error 22003. NaN equals nothing, itself included (NaN = NaN is FALSE, NaN <> 1 TRUE), and every ordering of NaN against a number is FALSE (NaN > 1, NaN <= NaN); against another kind it is NULL (NaN > 'a'): g.gql("WITH 0.0 / 0.0 AS n RETURN n = n AS a, n <> n AS b, n > 1 AS c, n > 'a' AS d, toString(1.0 / 0.0) AS e, toString(n) AS f").to_list() // #{"a": false, "b": true, "c": false, "d": (), "e": "Infinity", "f": "NaN"}
g.gql("RETURN 1 / 0 AS x").to_list() // error: division by zero (22012) The playground and the dev server show such a value as NaN, Infinity or -Infinity; JSON output writes it as a GraphSON typed double ( Displaying Results ). These rules follow Neo4j and the openCypher TCK, where ISO GQL calls a comparison of different kinds "not comparable" and raises 22012 for a division by zero of every numeric type ( differences from the standard ). No value is ever turned into text implicitly: 'a' + 1 is an error, not 'a1' (Neo4j converts here; Graphersal does not). Convert explicitly: 'a' || toString(1) or 'a' || CAST(1 AS STRING) ( Conversions ).

