The aggregates follow GQL and, where the standard text could not be checked yet, Neo4j: NULL is skipped by every aggregate except count(*), which counts rows. Typing. sum of integers is an integer, and an overflow is an error (22003), never a wrap; one float makes it a float. avg is always a float. sum and avg of a non-number (a string, a list) are an error (22G03); count and collect_list take any value. min / max compare across kinds in the order of ORDER BY (strings before numbers), never an error. Empty input. Without grouping keys, an aggregation of no rows still returns one row: count 0, sum 0, collect_list [], avg / min / max NULL (Neo4j's values; ISO GQL, from SQL, may give NULL for sum: provisional). With grouping keys there is no group, so no row. Reusing a key. An aggregating item may read a grouping key outside its aggregates by repeating it when the key is a variable or a property (RETURN p.age, p.age + count(*)), not when it is computed (RETURN a.age + b.age, a.age + b.age + count(*) is an error, 42002, as in Neo4j: the item reads a and b, which are no keys). Compute such a key in a WITH first and read it by name. Group order is unspecified (today: the order in which the groups first appear); write ORDER BY. GROUP BY after the RETURN items lists the grouping keys explicitly; grouping stays implicit, so it changes nothing in the result. A key names a column (GROUP BY name) or, beyond ISO GQL's references to variables, repeats an item's expression (GROUP BY p.name for RETURN p.name AS name, ...). g.gql("MATCH (n) RETURN count(n.age) AS ages, count(*) AS rows, collect_list(n.lang) AS langs").to_list()
// #{"ages": 4, "langs": ["java", "java"], "rows": 6}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) RETURN sum(n.age) AS i, sum(n.age * 1.0) AS f, avg(n.age) AS a").to_list() // #{"a": 30.75, "f": 123.0, "i": 123}
g.gql("MATCH (n) RETURN min(coalesce(n.age, n.lang)) AS lo, max(coalesce(n.age, n.lang)) AS hi").to_list() // #{"hi": 35, "lo": "java"}
g.gql("MATCH (n:robot) RETURN count(*) AS n, sum(n.age) AS s, avg(n.age) AS a, collect_list(n) AS l").to_list()
// #{"a": (), "l": [], "n": 0, "s": 0}
g.gql("MATCH (n:robot) RETURN n.name AS name, count(*) AS c").to_list() // no rows
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) RETURN sum(9223372036854775807) AS s") // error: numeric value out of range (22003) Gremlin differs here: values("age").sum() of nothing gives no result, count() counts what reaches it (Gremlin has no NULL property), and min() of mixed kinds is an error.

