x[i], x[a..b], m[k] and n[k] are Cypher forms with their Neo4j meaning (the openCypher TCK List1, List2, Map2 and Graph7 cases pass): A list index is an integer counted from 0; a negative one counts from the end (-1 is the last item). An index outside the list gives NULL, never an error. A float index is an error even when it is whole (x[1.0]), and so is a string, a boolean, a list or a map: convert with toInteger(i). A slice x[a..b] takes the items from a up to b, b excluded. A bound left out is the start (x[..b]) or the end (x[a..]); a negative bound counts from the end; bounds are clamped to the list, so [1, 2, 3][-5..5] is the whole list; a >= b gives []. A bound written as NULL (or a parameter that is NULL) makes the slice NULL. A map is read with a string key (case-sensitive; missing: NULL); a node or an edge with a string property key (missing: NULL). n['name'] with a string literal is exactly n.name. NULL as the list, map, element, index or bound gives NULL. Other kinds of value are not indexable: a string, a number, a boolean or a path gives 22G03 (a string is no list in GQL; use substring()). These errors arise when a row reaches the expression ( Type errors surface at evaluation time ). g.gql("RETURN [1, 2, 3][-5..5] AS a, [1, 2, 3][3..1] AS b, [1, 2, 3][1..NULL] AS c, {Name: 1}['name'] AS d").to_list()
