An f32 vector takes 4 bytes per component: 1.5 KiB for a 384-dimensional embedding. Declared with element: "i8" it takes 1 byte per component (plus 4 bytes for a scale), with almost the same search results (measured on the reference page ). The schema's element wins on write : a list or an f32 vector written to an i8 property is quantized as it is stored, so the host keeps sending what its model gives it: g.set_schema(#{mode: "open", vertices: #{doc: #{schema: #{type: "object", properties: #{ emb: #{type: "array", format: "vector", element: "i8", dimension: 3}}}}}});
for d in [ ["Cats and dogs", [0.9, 0.1, 0.0]], ["Pasta at home", [0.0, 0.9, 0.1]], ["Rust for beginners", [0.0, 0.0, 1.0]],
] { g.add_v("doc").property("title", d[0]).property("emb", d[1]).iterate();
} // Stored as i8: vector([0.9, 0.0992126, 0.0], "i8"); an f32 query searches it.
g.v().has("title", "Cats and dogs").values("emb").next();
g.v().has_label("doc").nearest("emb", [1.0, 0.2, 0.0], 1).values("title").next() // "Cats and dogs" The same rule works against you when you quantize a property that is already declared as f32 : set_schema/patch_schema to element: "i8" is refused while the stored vectors are still f32 (the schema would not fit the data), and a rewrite with quantize("i8") under the f32 declaration is turned straight back into an f32 vector. Switch the checks off for the migration, rewrite the values, then declare the new element: g.set_schema(#{mode: "open", vertices: #{doc: #{schema: #{type: "object", properties: #{ emb: #{type: "array", format: "vector", element: "f32", dimension: 3}}}}}});
g.add_v("doc").property("title", "Cats and dogs").property("emb", [0.9, 0.1, 0.0]).iterate();
g.add_v("doc").property("title", "Pasta at home").property("emb", [0.0, 0.9, 0.1]).iterate(); // 1. Mode "none": no checks, no conversion on write.
g.patch_schema(#{mode: "none"});
// 2. Rewrite every vector quantized.
g.v().has_label("doc").property("emb", __.values("emb").quantize("i8")).iterate();
// 3. Declare i8 and switch the checks on again: the data fits now.
g.patch_schema(#{mode: "open", vertices: #{doc: #{schema: #{properties: #{emb: #{element: "i8"}}}}}}); g.v().has_label("doc").values("emb").to_list().map(|v| v.element) // ["i8", "i8"] The playground runs a whole script as one unit, so a failure on the way leaves the graph as it was; from Python pass atomic=True to execute() for the same.

