Without ORDER BY the row order is unspecified : it may change with the data, the plan or the version. Write ORDER BY whenever the order matters (every example in this chapter does). NULL sorts last ascending and first descending (Neo4j's rule; GQL leaves it to the implementation). NULLS FIRST / NULLS LAST are not supported yet. Only the final ORDER BY orders the result. The order of a WITH ... ORDER BY decides what its OFFSET / LIMIT keep, but later clauses do not preserve it. Values of different types sort by type first, in this order (Neo4j's global order where Graphersal has the type): map, vertex, edge, list, date/time, UUID, string, boolean, number, vector, NULL. Within a type: numbers by value, strings by code point, elements by id, lists item by item and then by length. The sort is stable: rows with equal keys keep their relative order. g.gql("MATCH (n) RETURN n.name AS name, n.age AS age ORDER BY age, name").to_list()
// vadas 27, marko 29, josh 32, peter 35, lop (), ripple ()
g.gql("MATCH (n) RETURN n.name AS name, n.age AS age ORDER BY age DESC, name").to_list()
// lop (), ripple (), peter 35, josh 32, marko 29, vadas 27
g.gql("MATCH (n) RETURN coalesce(n.age, n.lang) AS v ORDER BY v").to_list()
// "java", "java", 27, 29, 32, 35: strings before numbers Gremlin's order().by("age") drops the vertices without age; ORDER BY keeps them.

