The functions ( The Supported Subset ) are pure: the same arguments always give the same result, so a LET or WITH name may stand for its expression anywhere. They follow these rules: Characters. Every position and length counts characters (Unicode scalar values), never bytes: char_length('héllo') is 5, byte_length('héllo') is 6, and left, right, substring, reverse and split(s, '') never cut a character in two. NULL in, NULL out. A function returns NULL when any argument is NULL, before it checks the kinds, except coalesce, nullif and the ...OrNull conversions. An unmatched OPTIONAL MATCH therefore gives NULL through every function. Wrong kind is an error. An argument of the wrong kind fails the query (22G03), never a silent NULL (only the ...OrNull conversions turn it into NULL). An argument that must be an integer (left, substring, range, the precision of round, the n of the list trim) does not take a float. No NaN or infinity from finite numbers. sqrt(-1) (2201F), ln(0) (2201E) and an exp() or power() that overflows (22003) are errors, like the arithmetic operators (whose one exception is a float divided by zero, IEEE 754: Comparisons across types ). A NaN that is already stored passes through abs, floor, ceil and round unchanged, and sign(NaN) is 0. Conversions are not arithmetic: toFloat('NaN') and toFloat('Infinity') give the float values, as in Neo4j. Result types. The GQL functions keep the argument's type: abs, floor and ceil of an integer are integers. round (Cypher) is always a float, sign always an integer; sqrt, exp, ln, log, log10 and power are floats; mod(a, b) is a % b, except that a float b = 0 is error 22012 (ISO MOD) where % gives NaN. Bounded results. A function whose result can be larger than its arguments (range, split, replace, CAST(x AS STRING) of a list or map, and the operator ||) computes the size of the result first and asks for it against traversal.max_materialized_bytes and the memory budget, like collect_list(). A result that is too large fails the query with the limit error; it is never cut short and never runs the process out of memory ( Errors ). No plan change. A condition with a function is evaluated by the GQL filter step and never pushed into has() or a property index: WHERE lower(n.name) = 'marko' scans the label. g.gql("MATCH (p:person) OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s:software {lang: 'none'}) RETURN p.name AS name, upper(s.name) AS u, toString(s) AS t, head(s.tags) AS h ORDER BY name LIMIT 1").to_list()
// #{"h": (), "name": "josh", "t": (), "u": ()}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN left(n.name, 2.0) AS x") // error: left() takes an integer length (22G03)

