round(x [, precision [, mode]]) follows Neo4j and always returns a float: One argument, or precision 0 without a mode: Java's Math.round, a half rounds toward positive infinity (round(2.5) is 3.0, round(-2.5) and round(-2.5, 0) are -2.0). With a positive precision, or with a mode: the shortest decimal text of x is rounded (Java's BigDecimal.valueOf), so round(2.675, 2) is 2.68 although the float 2.675 is a bit below it. The default mode is 'HALF_UP' (a half away from zero: round(-1.55, 1) is -1.6). mode is one of 'UP', 'DOWN', 'CEILING', 'FLOOR', 'HALF_UP', 'HALF_DOWN', 'HALF_EVEN', written in upper case (as Neo4j, which uses Java's mode names); any other text, Java's 'UNNECESSARY' included, is 22023. A negative precision is 22023 too, and a float precision 22G03. NaN and the infinities pass through. A number beyond the integer range is rounded as it is, never clamped: round(1.0e20) is 1.0e20. Gremlin's math() has no round; the logarithm of math("log(_)") is the natural one, the same as ln(x) and the one-argument log(x). log(2, x) and log(10, x) are exact for powers of the base (log(2, 1024) is 10.0). g.gql("MATCH (n:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN round(-1.5) AS a, round(-1.5, 0) AS b, round(-1.5, 0, 'HALF_UP') AS c, round(1.25, 1, 'HALF_EVEN') AS d").to_list()
// #{"a": -1.0, "b": -1.0, "c": -2.0, "d": 1.2}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN log(2, 1024) AS a, log(10, 0.001) AS b").to_list() // #{"a": 10.0, "b": -3.0}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN round(1.5, 0, 'half_up') AS x") // error: round() has no rounding mode "half_up" (22023)

