Trim. Every GQL trim form removes the space (U+0020) when no character is given: TRIM(s), TRIM(LEADING FROM s), trim(s), btrim(s), ltrim(s), rtrim(s). A tab or a line break stays. TRIM(c FROM s) takes exactly one character c (22027 otherwise); btrim(s, chars), ltrim(s, chars) and rtrim(s, chars) remove any character of the set chars ('' removes nothing). trim(s, x) with a string s is an error that names btrim(s, chars); trim(list, n) is GQL's list trim. Split. Empty parts are kept: split('a,,b,', ',') is ['a', '', 'b', ''], and split('', ',') is ['']. An empty delimiter splits into characters (split('', '') is ['']). With a list of delimiters the text is cut at the leftmost match, and when two delimiters match at the same place the one listed first wins (split('a--b', ['-', '--']) is ['a', '', 'b'], split('a--b', ['--', '-']) is ['a', 'b']); a list containing '' splits into characters, a NULL in the list gives NULL, and [] gives [s]. Replace. Every non-overlapping occurrence, left to right. An empty search inserts the replacement before every character and at the end (replace('abc', '', '-') is '-a-b-c-', Java's rule). The optional fourth argument limit (Neo4j 2025.06) replaces at most that many occurrences, from the left (0 changes nothing, a negative limit is 22023). Gremlin's string steps differ in these places, so the same-named Gremlin step is a twin only for plain inputs: g.gql("MATCH (n:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN split('a,,b', ',') AS s").to_list() // #{"s": ["a", "", "b"]}
g.inject("a,,b").split(",").to_list() // ["a", "b"]: Gremlin drops the empty part
g.gql("MATCH (n:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN replace('abc', '', '-') AS r").to_list() // #{"r": "-a-b-c-"}
g.inject("abc").replace("", "-").to_list() // "abc": Gremlin leaves the text unchanged
g.gql("MATCH (n:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN trim(' \t a \t ') AS t").to_list() // the tabs stay: "\t a \t"
g.inject(" \t a \t ").trim().to_list() // "a": Gremlin's trim() removes all whitespace

