The temporal functions are openCypher's, with Neo4j's meaning, on the engine's date and time values . Three properties of those values shape the answers: Microseconds. A value holds microseconds; Neo4j holds nanoseconds. gql() never cuts digits: text, a map field or a computed duration with a non-zero part below the microsecond is 0A000 "not yet". The Rhai constructors cut such digits ( Text Formats ); a value built in Rhai therefore reaches gql() already cut. Offsets, not zone names. A datetime keeps a fixed UTC offset. A zone name (in text, [Europe/London], or as timezone: 'Europe/Stockholm') resolves to the zone's offset at that instant, and the name is not kept: d.timezone gives the offset text, and later arithmetic stays at that offset, with no step at a daylight saving change ( Time Zones ). One clock per query. The constructors without an argument, X.statement() and X.transaction() all read one instant, taken when the query starts; X.realtime() is refused, as rand() is. The Gremlin steps and the Rhai operators keep their own (PostgreSQL-style) rules: every difference below lives in gql() only. Dates in GQL sets each function beside the Gremlin step that does the same. g.gql("RETURN datetime('2015-07-21T21:40:32.142[Europe/London]') AS d, datetime({year: 1984, month: 10, day: 11, hour: 12, timezone: 'Europe/Stockholm'}).timezone AS z").to_list()
// #{"d": datetime("2015-07-21T21:40:32.142+01:00"), "z": "+01:00"}
g.gql("RETURN datetime('2015-03-29T01:30+01:00') + duration('PT1H') AS d").to_list() // #{"d": datetime("2015-03-29T02:30:00+01:00")}
g.gql("RETURN localtime({hour: 12, nanosecond: 5}) AS t") // error: a part below the microsecond is not yet supported

