Every result row of gql() is a map from column name to value, whatever the plan behind it (the planner may start a pattern at any of its nodes; the row looks the same). RETURN is mandatory in a query that only reads, as in GQL and Cypher: such a query without it is a syntax error whose help suggests RETURN * or RETURN a, b. A query whose last clause writes (or that ends with FINISH) returns no rows ( Writing with GQL ). g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS name, p.age AS age ORDER BY age").to_list()
// #{"age": 27, "name": "vadas"}, #{"age": 29, "name": "marko"}, #{"age": 32, "name": "josh"}, #{"age": 35, "name": "peter"}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person)") // error: missing RETURN A value is a property value (number, string, boolean, date, UUID, vector, ...), NULL (a missing property gives a NULL column, the key is never left out), a list, a map (both may nest ), a vertex or edge, or a path . An element column holds the live element, so Gremlin can continue from it: g.gql("MATCH (a)-[e]->(b) RETURN * ORDER BY element_id(e) LIMIT 2").to_list()
// #{"a": v[1], "b": v[2], "e": e[0][1-knows->2]}, #{"a": v[1], "b": v[3], "e": e[1][1-created->3]}
g.gql("MATCH (s:software) RETURN s ORDER BY s.name").select("s").in("created").values("name").order().to_list() // "josh", "josh", "marko", "peter"

