The engine keeps the columns in the written order (RETURN p.name AS name, p.age AS age gives name, then age). Whether you see that order depends on where the rows go: Output Column order JSON: to_json(), --format json, the dev server's JSON API, the MCP tools gql and query written order Rust: the row is an ElementProperty::Map written order Rhai maps (to_list(), next()), the REPL / CLI table view sorted by key Python dicts sorted by key Rhai object maps and Python dicts built from them are sorted by key, the same as for Gremlin's project() today; the table view shows their columns in that order. g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS name, p.age AS age ORDER BY age LIMIT 2").to_json()
// [{"name": "vadas", "age": 27}, {"name": "marko", "age": 29}]
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS name, p.age AS age ORDER BY age").next().keys() // "age", "name" When the written order matters, read the rows as JSON or name the columns so that their alphabetical order is the order you want.

