A column may hold maps and lists nested to any depth: a record constructor {k: expr}, a map projection p {.name, ...} and a list or pattern comprehension build them per row, so one row can carry an entity with its related data inside. Keys keep their written order in the JSON output, at every level, like the columns of a row ( Column order ); Rhai maps sort them by key as anywhere else. g.gql("MATCH (s:software) RETURN s {.name, creators: [(s)<-[:created]-(p) | p {.name, .age}]} AS sw ORDER BY sw.name").to_json()
// [{"sw": {"name": "lop", "creators": [{"name": "peter", "age": 35}, {"name": "josh", "age": 32}, {"name": "marko", "age": 29}]}},
// {"sw": {"name": "ripple", "creators": [{"name": "josh", "age": 32}]}}]
// (the creators of one program come in no defined order) A vertex, edge or path inside a map or a list stays the live element, as in a column of its own: Gremlin can continue from it, and JSON renders it as the element object {id, label, properties} (an edge also its endpoints, inV and outV). Project the properties you need (f {.name} instead of f) for a smaller answer. g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'})-[:knows]->(f {name: 'vadas'}) RETURN p {.name, friend: f} AS x").to_list() // #{"x": #{"friend": v[2], "name": "marko"}}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'})-[:knows]->(f {name: 'vadas'}) RETURN p {.name, friend: f} AS x").to_json()
// [{"x": {"name": "marko", "friend": {"id": "2", "label": "person", "properties": {"name": "vadas", "age": 27}}}}]
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN [(p)-[:knows]->(f) | f] AS friends").select("friends").unfold().values("name").order().to_list() // "josh", "vadas" Value Rhai / Rust JSON a map projection or record a map (ElementProperty::Map), keys in written order in Rust an object, keys in written order a map projection of a NULL variable () / NULL null a list or pattern comprehension a list; [] when nothing is kept or matched an array an element inside either the live vertex or edge the element object

