INSERT takes one or more comma-separated patterns. A node pattern creates a node with the labels written (none, one, or several joined by &; Cypher's :A:B is the alias) and the properties of its map. An edge pattern creates an edge between the nodes beside it: it needs a direction (-[:T]-> or <-[:T]-) and exactly one label. g.gql("INSERT (:city {name: 'Berlin', population: 3.7})").iterate();
g.gql("INSERT (a:person {name: 'ann'})-[:knows {since: 2020}]->(b:person&admin {name: 'bob'})").iterate();
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'ann'})-[e:knows]->(b) RETURN e.since AS since, labels(b) AS labels").to_list() // #{"labels": ["person", "admin"], "since": 2020} The same in Gremlin: g.add_v("person").property("name", "ann").as("a").add_v(["person", "admin"]).property("name", "bob").add_e("knows").from("a").property("since", 2020).count().next() // 1 A query may end with the INSERT: it then returns no rows . GQL's FINISH ends a query without a result too. RETURN after an INSERT reads the new elements, and RETURN * includes the variables the INSERT named: g.gql("INSERT (c:city {name: 'Berlin'}) RETURN c.name AS name").to_list() // #{"name": "Berlin"}
g.gql("CREATE (c:city:capital {name: 'Rome'}) RETURN labels(c) AS l").to_list() // #{"l": ["city", "capital"]}
g.gql("INSERT (:city {name: 'Paris'})").to_list() // no rows
g.gql("INSERT (:city {name: 'Paris'}) FINISH").to_list() // no rows Property values are expressions: literals, $parameters, functions and the variables of the row. A property whose value is NULL is not stored (GQL has no stored NULL; a Gremlin property(k, ()) stores one). Lists and maps are stored as they are, also lists of mixed kinds (a difference from Neo4j ); a node, an edge or a path cannot be a property value (22G03). g.gql("INSERT (n:x {k: NULL, tags: ['a', 1], address: {city: 'Rome'}}) RETURN keys(n) AS keys, n.address.city AS city").to_list() // #{"city": "Rome", "keys": ["tags", "address"]} Pattern rules. A variable bound before the INSERT is written alone, (p): it connects the existing node and may not carry labels or properties (42002; change an existing node with SET , as in MATCH (p:person) SET p:admin). An edge without a direction, an edge with a label expression (:r|s), a node label expression other than a conjunction, a path variable (p = ...) and WHERE or a quantifier inside the pattern are errors before the run; the help shows the accepted form. g.gql("INSERT (a:x)-[:r]-(b:y)") // error: INSERT needs a direction
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) INSERT (p:admin)") // error: the variable `p` is already bound

