SET takes one or more comma-separated items and applies them to the elements of each row that reaches it, left to right, so a later item sees the earlier ones: Item Effect SET n.k = expr (also SET (n).k = expr) sets one property of the node or edge n SET n = {k: expr, ...} replaces every property: the keys missing from the map are removed SET n += {k: expr, ...} merges the map in (a Cypher compatibility form ): other keys stay SET n:A / SET n IS A adds the label A to the node n; SET n:A:B and SET n IS A & B add both g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) SET p.age = p.age + 1, p.checked = true RETURN p.age AS age, p.checked AS checked").to_list() // #{"age": 30, "checked": true}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) SET p:admin:staff RETURN labels(p) AS labels").to_list() // #{"labels": ["person", "admin", "staff"]}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) SET p += {city: 'Rome'} RETURN properties(p) AS props").to_list() // #{"props": #{"age": 29, "city": "Rome", "name": "marko"}}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) SET p = {name: 'marko', city: 'Rome'} RETURN properties(p) AS props").to_list() // #{"props": #{"city": "Rome", "name": "marko"}}
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[e:knows]->(b) SET e.checked = true RETURN b.name AS b, e.checked AS checked ORDER BY b").to_list() // josh true, vadas true NULL removes. GQL stores no NULL, so SET n.k = NULL removes the property, and a NULL value in the map of SET n = {..} or SET n += {..} removes its key. A NULL target (a variable an OPTIONAL MATCH left NULL) is skipped: the item does nothing for that row. A NULL map on the right of = or += is an error (22004): write SET n = {} to remove every property, or coalesce($map, {}) for a parameter that may be NULL. g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) SET p.age = NULL RETURN keys(p) AS keys").to_list() // #{"keys": ["name"]}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) SET p += {age: NULL, city: 'Rome'} RETURN properties(p) AS props").to_list() // #{"props": #{"city": "Rome", "name": "marko"}}
g.gql("OPTIONAL MATCH (p:person {name: 'nobody'}) SET p.seen = true RETURN p").to_list() // #{"p": ()}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) SET p = $props", #{props: ()}) // error: the right side of SET p = .. is NULL, not a map Values follow the rules of INSERT: literals, $parameters, functions and the row's variables; lists and maps are stored as they are; a node, an edge or a path cannot be a property value (22G03). Every value of a map is checked before the item writes anything. On the right of = and += a node or an edge stands for its properties (SET n = m copies them, SET n = n changes nothing); any other value that is not a map is an error (22G03). An aggregate cannot be a value: aggregate in a WITH first. g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}), (q:person {name: 'josh'}) SET p = q RETURN properties(p) AS props").to_list() // #{"props": #{"age": 32, "name": "josh"}}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) WITH p, count(s) AS n SET p.projects = n RETURN p.name AS name, p.projects AS n ORDER BY name").to_list()
// josh 2, marko 1, peter 1, vadas 0
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) SET p.self = p") // error: a property cannot hold a graph element Labels. A label item changes a node's label set: adding a label it has, like removing one it lacks, is no change. An edge has exactly one label, its type, so a label item on an edge is an error: 42002 before the run when the variable is an edge of a pattern, 22G03 when a row holds an edge (Gremlin's set_label() changes an edge's label). The item names labels joined by : or &; |, ! and % are syntax errors. g.gql("MATCH (a)-[e:knows]->(b) SET e:friend") // error: an edge has exactly one label Targets. The target of an item is a variable of the row: n.k, (n).k or n. A deeper target (n.address.city = .., head(list).k = ..) is not supported yet (0A000); name the element first, as in WITH head(list) AS x SET x.k = ... The variable must be bound before the SET (42002).

