DELETE takes one or more comma-separated expressions and removes the nodes and edges they give, for every row that reaches it. DETACH DELETE removes a node together with all its edges. A plain DELETE (GQL's default, also written NODETACH DELETE ) removes only nodes without edges: a node that still has an edge fails the query (G1001) and nothing is removed. Edges the same clause deletes do not count, so DELETE e, n removes a node and the edge that held it in one clause. g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) DETACH DELETE p").iterate();
[g.v().count().next(), g.e().count().next()] // [5, 3] g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[e:knows]->(b {name: 'vadas'}) DELETE e RETURN b.name AS still_here").to_list() // #{"still_here": "vadas"}
g.gql("MATCH (s:software {name: 'ripple'})<-[e]-() DELETE e, s RETURN count(*) AS n").to_list() // #{"n": 1}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) DELETE p") // error: `p` is a node that still has edges (G1001) The NODETACH check runs for the whole clause before it removes anything: the help of the error names the DETACH DELETE that works, built from the failing query. Targets are expressions: a node or an edge variable, a path (MATCH p = .. DETACH DELETE p removes its nodes and edges), a list of them (also nested, such as collect(n)), or an expression that gives one (a map member, head(list)). A NULL target (an OPTIONAL MATCH that found nothing) is skipped, and an element already removed (by an earlier row or clause) is not removed twice. A target that gives another value fails the query: before the run when the text shows it (DELETE 1 + 1, 42002), on the row otherwise (22G03). A label test is not a target: DELETE n:L is a syntax error whose help names REMOVE n:L, which removes the label and keeps the node. g.gql("MATCH p = (:person {name: 'marko'})-[:knows]->(:person {name: 'vadas'}) DETACH DELETE p").iterate();
[g.v().count().next(), g.e().count().next()] // [4, 3] g.gql("OPTIONAL MATCH (p:person {name: 'nobody'}) DELETE p RETURN p").to_list() // #{"p": ()}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WITH collect(p) AS people DETACH DELETE people RETURN size(people) AS n").to_list() // #{"n": 4}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) DELETE n:person") // error: a label test is not an element; REMOVE n:person removes the label After a DELETE. A deleted element is gone for the rest of the query: reading its properties, labels, element_id() or endpoints is an error , never a silent NULL, and so is returning it (RETURN n, also with LIMIT 0 after it, and RETURN p of a path holding it). Read what you need before the DELETE and carry it in a WITH; count(n) and n IS NULL still work, and type(e) of a deleted edge still gives its label (as in the openCypher tests). A walk from a deleted node (MATCH (n)-->(m) after deleting n) finds nothing. g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'vadas'}) WITH p, properties(p) AS props DETACH DELETE p RETURN props").to_list() // #{"props": #{"age": 27, "name": "vadas"}}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'vadas'}) DETACH DELETE p RETURN count(p) AS deleted").to_list() // #{"deleted": 1}
g.gql("MATCH (a)-[e:created]->(s:software {name: 'lop'}) DELETE e RETURN type(e) AS t, a.name AS who ORDER BY who").to_list() // three rows, t "created"
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'vadas'}) DETACH DELETE p RETURN p") // error: the node was deleted earlier in this query
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'vadas'}) DETACH DELETE p RETURN p.name AS name") // error: the node was deleted earlier in this query Returning a deleted node is an error in gql() where Neo4j returns a value of it; the reasons are on Semantics . A query that fails after its DELETE changed nothing ( Units ): the removed elements come back with their ids, labels, properties and edges. In Rust they are new elements: a VertexId / EdgeId handle taken before the failed run is stale and must be looked up again by id ( What a rollback restores ), exactly as after a failing Gremlin drop(). A Rhai vertex or edge value finds the element by its id and stays usable. The same in Gremlin: g.v().has("name", "marko").drop().count().next() // 0
g.v().has("name", "marko").out_e("knows").where(__.in_v().has("name", "vadas")).drop().iterate() drop() of a vertex always removes its edges (it is DETACH DELETE); the NODETACH check has no Gremlin twin.

447.body:
A write clause runs once for each row that reaches it, so the clauses before it decide how many elements an INSERT creates and how often a SET applies (SET n.c = n.c + 1 over two rows of the same node adds 2). A MATCH gives one row per match, FOR (Cypher UNWIND) one per list item, and a variable of the row connects the new elements to what it matched: g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) INSERT (p)-[:likes]->(f:fruit {name: 'apple'}) RETURN f.name AS fruit").to_list(); // #{"fruit": "apple"}
g.gql("FOR n IN $names INSERT (:person {name: n, born: $year})", #{names: ["ann", "bob"], year: 1990}).iterate();
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE p.born = 1990 RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // #{"name": "ann"}, #{"name": "bob"} A write clause may stand after MATCH, OPTIONAL MATCH, FOR, WITH, LET, FILTER and another write clause, and further clauses may follow it: a MATCH after the INSERT sees the new elements, and a new node can be connected to every row a later MATCH finds: g.gql("INSERT (c:city {name: 'Berlin'}) MATCH (p:person) INSERT (p)-[:lives_in]->(c) RETURN count(*) AS n").to_list() // #{"n": 4}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WITH p, p.age + 1 AS later INSERT (p)-[:turns]->(:age {years: later}) RETURN p.name AS name, later ORDER BY name").to_list()
// #{"later": 33, "name": "josh"}, #{"later": 30, "name": "marko"}, #{"later": 36, "name": "peter"}, #{"later": 28, "name": "vadas"} A node an OPTIONAL MATCH left NULL cannot be an edge's endpoint: the query fails (22004) and changes nothing. Keep only the bound rows with FILTER x IS NOT NULL first: g.gql("OPTIONAL MATCH (p:person {name: 'nobody'}) INSERT (p)-[:knows]->(:person)") // error: the source node `p` of the inserted `:knows` edge is NULL
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:created]->(s) FILTER s IS NOT NULL INSERT (p)-[:made]->(s) RETURN count(*) AS n").to_list() // #{"n": 4} Inside CALL. A CALL body may end with its write instead of RETURN (a unit subquery): it runs once per incoming row and passes the row on unchanged, also when the body found nothing: g.gql("MATCH (p:person) CALL (p) { INSERT (p)-[:likes]->(:fruit {name: p.name}) } RETURN count(*) AS c").to_list() // #{"c": 4} A subquery expression (EXISTS { }, COUNT { }, COLLECT { }) and a comprehension cannot write (42001), and a write in an operand of UNION is not supported yet.

450.body:
An INSERT creates each element in one storage call with all its properties, so a schema sees the whole element ( Schemas ): defaults on create are filled in, a required property is satisfied by the map, write-time coercion applies, and a closed schema refuses undeclared labels and properties. SET and REMOVE are checked like the Gremlin steps that do the same: a value of the wrong type, removing a required property (also with SET n.k = NULL or a SET n = {..} without it) and a label the schema does not allow fail the query. A violation fails the query (and changes nothing), with a help line written for GQL: g.set_schema(#{mode: "open", vertices: #{person: #{schema: #{type: "object", properties: #{ name: #{type: "string"}, age: #{type: "integer"}, active: #{type: "boolean", default: true}}, required: ["name"]}}}});
g.gql("INSERT (:person {name: 'alice'})").iterate();
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS name, p.active AS active").to_list() // #{"active": true, "name": "alice"} Caused by: Missing required property 'name' on vertex 'person'
Help: Give 'name' a value in the property map of the INSERT, as in g.gql("INSERT (:person {name: 'value'})"); a NULL value (such as a property the matched element lacks) is not stored and so is missing too: give it a fallback, as in {name: coalesce(n.name, 'unknown')}. Or remove it from the label's "required" in the schema, or give it a "default" (or "defaultFunction") in the schema: it is stored when an element is created without the property. A MERGE that creates creates like an INSERT of its pattern: the schema's defaults are filled in and the pattern's map must satisfy required. Its ON CREATE SET items run afterwards, as a second write in the same query, so a required property given only there is missing when the element is created and fails the query; put it into the pattern's map instead (where it is also part of what MERGE looks for): g.set_schema(#{mode: "open", vertices: #{person: #{schema: #{type: "object", properties: #{ name: #{type: "string"}, active: #{type: "boolean", default: true}}, required: ["name"]}}}});
g.gql("MERGE (p:person {name: 'ann'}) RETURN p.active AS active").to_list() // #{"active": true} A value of the wrong type is not converted, and GQL never infers a type from text. Convert it where the INSERT writes it: Caused by: Type mismatch on vertex 'person.age': expected int64, got string
Help: Convert the value to the type 'int64' where the INSERT writes it, with toInteger(), as in g.gql("INSERT (:person {age: toInteger($v)})", #{v: "29"}). toInteger() gives NULL for a value it cannot convert, and a NULL value is not stored; CAST(x AS ..) fails the query for it instead.

452.body:
A saved query may be written in GQL itself ( Saved queries in GQL ): the body is the plain query text, its $parameters are the declared ones, and an INSERT, SET, REMOVE, DELETE or MERGE makes it a query that writes, whose effects name what its plan creates, changes and deletes. A call writes in one unit and returns the rows. A GQL query calls it with CALL hire('lop', 'ann') YIELD name (the arguments in the order the body first reads them: $company, then $name); its writes then belong to the calling query's unit ( Calling saved queries ): g.define_query(#{name: "hire", language: "gql", params: #{name: "string", company: "string"}, body: "MATCH (c:software {name: $company}) INSERT (p:person {name: $name})-[:created]->(c) RETURN p.name AS name"});
g.get_query("hire")["effects"].map(|e| e.text); // ["Create Data(vertex \"person\")", "Create Data(edge \"created\")"]
g.query("hire", #{name: "ann", company: "lop"}).select("name").to_list() // "ann" g.define_query(#{name: "birthday", language: "gql", params: #{name: "string"}, body: "MATCH (p:person {name: $name}) SET p.age = p.age + 1 RETURN p.age AS age"});
g.get_query("birthday")["effects"].map(|e| e.text); // ["Update Data(vertex)"]
g.query("birthday", #{name: "vadas"}).select("age").to_list() // 28 g.define_query(#{name: "unlink", language: "gql", params: #{}, body: "MATCH ()-[e:knows]->() DELETE e"});
g.get_query("unlink")["effects"].map(|e| e.text) // ["Delete Data(edge \"knows\")"] g.define_query(#{name: "visit", language: "gql", params: #{name: "string"}, body: "MERGE (p:person {name: $name}) ON MATCH SET p.visits = coalesce(p.visits, 0) + 1 RETURN p.name AS name"});
g.get_query("visit")["effects"].map(|e| e.text) // ["Create Data(vertex \"person\")", "Update Data(vertex)"] A saved query in Rhai whose body runs g.gql("INSERT ...") with a literal query text is a writing query too: its effects name what it creates, changes and deletes. A computed query text claims every write; pass values as $parameters instead. In a script, a writing gql() takes the write lock like a Gremlin write (is_mutating() is true), and the playground asks before running it again. Gremlin steps may follow: g.gql("INSERT (c:city {name: 'Berlin'}) RETURN c").select("c").values("name").to_list() // "Berlin"

453.body:
Run a writing query on the writing source, traversal_mut(), under the write lock. The read source refuses it with the error a Gremlin write gets there: use graphersal::prelude::*; let graph = Graph::new(TraversalGraph::tinkerpop_modern());
graph .write() .traversal_mut() .gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) INSERT (p)-[:likes]->(:fruit {name: 'apple'})") .iterate()?; // The read source cannot write:
let error = graph.read().traversal().gql("INSERT (:x)").iterate().unwrap_err();
assert_eq!( error.root_cause().to_string(), "Invalid element state: Cannot mutate immutable graph reference"
);

