A Rhai backtick string may span lines and processes no backslash escapes, so a multi-line query reads as it would in any GQL editor. Line breaks are whitespace for GQL; the error positions count lines and columns inside the query. g.gql(`MATCH (p:person)-[:created]->(s:software) WHERE p.age > 30 RETURN p.name AS who, s.name AS what ORDER BY who, what`).to_list()
// #{"what": "lop", "who": "josh"}, #{"what": "ripple", "who": "josh"}, #{"what": "lop", "who": "peter"} Inside a backtick string both GQL quote forms work unescaped ('lop' and "lop"). Two characters are special to Rhai there: ${ starts a Rhai interpolation: ${expr} is replaced by the value of a Rhai expression. GQL never needs ${, so this only matters when you use it on purpose (next section). A $ followed by a name, a GQL parameter such as $min, is left alone. A backtick ends the string. A backtick that GQL should see is doubled : Rhai reads `` inside a backtick string as one backtick. GQL back-quoted identifiers are therefore written with doubled backticks there: g.gql(`MATCH (s:software) RETURN s.name AS ``the name`` ORDER BY ``the name```).to_list() // #{"the name": "lop"}, #{"the name": "ripple"} The same query in a "..." string needs no doubling, because a backtick means nothing to a Rhai double-quoted string: g.gql("MATCH (s:software) RETURN s.name AS `the name` ORDER BY `the name`").to_list() // #{"the name": "lop"}, #{"the name": "ripple"}
g.gql("MATCH (`my var`:software) RETURN `my var`.name AS n ORDER BY n").to_list() // #{"n": "lop"}, #{"n": "ripple"}

459.body:
Put values into a query as parameters : $name in the query, the value in the map after it. A parameter is a typed value that can never change the query text, whatever it contains: g.gql(`MATCH (s:software) WHERE s.lang = $lang RETURN s.name AS name ORDER BY name`, #{lang: "java"}).to_list() // #{"name": "lop"}, #{"name": "ripple"}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) WHERE n.age > $min RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name", #{min: 30}).to_list() // #{"name": "josh"}, #{"name": "peter"} Interpolation (${..}) and string concatenation ("..." + x + "...") paste the value in as query text . It runs, but a value with a quote in it breaks the query (or changes it), a number becomes text, and nothing is checked: { let name = "o'brien"; g.gql(`MATCH (p:person) WHERE p.name = '${name}' RETURN p.age AS age`).to_list() } // error: unterminated string literal
{ let name = "o'brien"; g.gql(`MATCH (p:person) WHERE p.name = $name RETURN p.age AS age`, #{name: name}).to_list() } // no rows, no error In the first query the quote in the value ends the GQL string literal early; in the second the value is compared as it is. Parameters also keep the plan reusable and let a property index serve the lookup ( Index use ). A parameter name that is not an identifier is back-quoted: $`my value` (in a backtick string $``my value``).

460.body:
GQL has three comment forms, and all of them work inside both Rhai string forms (Rhai does not look for comments inside a string): // ... to the end of the line; /* ... */, not nested; -- ... to the end of the line, the ISO GQL form. g.gql(`-- people older than 30
MATCH (p:person) // every person WHERE p.age > 30 /* only the older ones */
RETURN p.name AS name -- one column
ORDER BY name`).to_list()
// #{"name": "josh"}, #{"name": "peter"} The -- rule. -- is a comment in GQL but an edge in Cypher ((a)--(b)). Graphersal reads -- as an edge when the next character is > or ( (-->, --(), or when the token before it is a ) on the same line ((a)--(b), (a) -- (b)); everywhere else it starts a comment. So a -- comment on a line of its own or after a value is a comment, and an edge pattern is never misread. The one trap: a -- comment written right after a node pattern on the same line becomes an edge and the query fails loudly; use // there. g.gql(`MATCH (a:person)--(b:software) RETURN a.name AS a, b.name AS b ORDER BY a, b`).to_list()
// josh/lop, josh/ripple, marko/lop, peter/lop g.gql(`MATCH (p:person) -- every person RETURN p.name AS name`) // error: unexpected `every`: this -- is read as an edge

