gql() speaks ISO GQL. Common Cypher spellings are accepted as aliases where they are unambiguous and mean exactly the same: an alias is translated to its GQL form while the query is parsed, so both spellings give the same plan and the same rows. An alias never has a meaning of its own. This table lists every accepted alias; Cypher functions without any GQL counterpart are the compatibility functions below, and Cypher's map projections and comprehensions, COUNT { }, COLLECT { }, SET n += .. and MERGE the compatibility forms ( Map projections and comprehensions , COUNT and COLLECT , SET and REMOVE , MERGE ); any other Cypher spelling is either not supported yet or refused . Cypher spelling GQL form Notes SKIP n OFFSET n a != b a <> b s1 + s2 on two strings s1 || s2 also on two lists; + of a string and a number is an error (no implicit conversion: write s + toString(n) or s || CAST(n AS STRING)) (n:A:B) (n:A&B) plain names only; mixing :A:B with |, & or ! is an error (write it with &) -[:A|:B]-> -[:A|B]-> a colon after the | of a label disjunction n:A in an expression n IS LABELED A --> -> abbreviated edge pointing right <-- <- abbreviated edge pointing left -- - abbreviated edge, either direction ( the -- rule ) <--> <-> either direction elementId(x) element_id(x) the id as a string toUpper(s) upper(s) toLower(s) lower(s) size(s) on a string char_length(s) size(list) is the item count, as cardinality(list) collect(x) collect_list(x) also collect(DISTINCT x) percentileCont(x, p), percentileDisc(x, p) percentile_cont(x, p), percentile_disc(x, p) ISO's binary set functions, value first ( Aggregation ) length(p) path_length(p) the number of edges of a path log(x) (one argument) ln(x) the natural logarithm; the two-argument log(base, x) is GQL's general logarithm -[e:T*]-> -[e:T]->{1,} Cypher's lower bound is 1: not GQL's postfix *, which is {0,} -[e:T*n]-> -[e:T]->{n} -[e:T*m..n]-> -[e:T]->{m,n} -[e:T*..n]-> -[e:T]->{1,n} lower bound 1 again -[e:T*m..]-> -[e:T]->{m,} -[e:T*m..n]-> with m > n, -[e:T*..0]-> no match the empty interval matches nothing (no row; OPTIONAL MATCH gives its NULL row); the GQL {3,1} is a syntax error a < b <= c, a chained comparison a < b AND b <= c any mix of =, <>, <, <=, >, >=; the middle operand is evaluated once per comparison WHERE (a)-[:T]->(b), a pattern predicate WHERE EXISTS { (a)-[:T]->(b) } where a condition stands (WHERE, FILTER, under NOT / AND / OR / XOR, a comprehension's WHERE, a searched CASE WHEN; elsewhere 42001); it may name only variables bound before it ( EXISTS ) UNWIND list AS x FOR x IN list one row per item; WITH ORDINALITY / WITH OFFSET only after FOR CREATE <pattern> INSERT <pattern> the same clause: once per row, nodes with label conjunctions (:A:B as :A&B), directed edges with one label ( Writing with GQL ); CREATE INDEX / CONSTRAINT / GRAPH are refused catalog commands CALL { WITH a, b ... }, the importing WITH CALL (a, b) { ... } the first WITH of the body, when it lists plain variables only, is the variable scope clause ( CALL subqueries ) SET n:A:B, REMOVE n:A:B SET n:A, n:B, REMOVE n:A, n:B the labels in order; SET n IS A & B likewise ( Writing with GQL ) SET (n).k = v, REMOVE (n).k SET n.k = v, REMOVE n.k a parenthesized variable as the target localtime(x), localdatetime(x), datetime(x) local_time(x), local_datetime(x), zoned_datetime(x) the temporal constructors ( Dates and times ) Each pair below returns the same rows: g.gql("MATCH (n:person) RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name SKIP 2").to_list() // #{"name": "peter"}, #{"name": "vadas"}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name OFFSET 2").to_list() // #{"name": "peter"}, #{"name": "vadas"}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) WHERE n.age != 29 RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // josh, peter, vadas
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) WHERE n.age <> 29 RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // josh, peter, vadas
g.gql("MATCH (p {name: 'marko'})-->(x) RETURN x.name AS x ORDER BY x").to_list() // josh, lop, vadas
g.gql("MATCH (p {name: 'marko'})->(x) RETURN x.name AS x ORDER BY x").to_list() // josh, lop, vadas
g.gql("MATCH (n) WHERE n:software RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // lop, ripple
g.gql("MATCH (n) WHERE n IS LABELED software RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // lop, ripple
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) RETURN elementId(n) AS id, toUpper(n.name) AS up, size(n.name) AS len, n.name + '?' AS q ORDER BY id LIMIT 1").to_list()
// #{"id": "1", "len": 5, "q": "marko?", "up": "MARKO"}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) RETURN element_id(n) AS id, upper(n.name) AS up, char_length(n.name) AS len, n.name || '?' AS q ORDER BY id LIMIT 1").to_list()
// #{"id": "1", "len": 5, "q": "marko?", "up": "MARKO"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person)-[:created]->(s) RETURN p.name AS name, collect(s.name) AS sw ORDER BY name").to_list()
// #{"name": "josh", "sw": ["ripple", "lop"]}, #{"name": "marko", "sw": ["lop"]}, #{"name": "peter", "sw": ["lop"]}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person)-[:created]->(s) RETURN p.name AS name, collect_list(s.name) AS sw ORDER BY name").to_list()
// #{"name": "josh", "sw": ["ripple", "lop"]}, #{"name": "marko", "sw": ["lop"]}, #{"name": "peter", "sw": ["lop"]}
g.gql("MATCH p = (a {name: 'marko'})-[*1..2]->(b) RETURN b.name AS b, length(p) AS len ORDER BY len, b").to_list()
// josh 1, lop 1, vadas 1, lop 2, ripple 2
g.gql("MATCH p = (a {name: 'marko'})-[]->{1,2}(b) RETURN b.name AS b, path_length(p) AS len ORDER BY len, b").to_list()
// josh 1, lop 1, vadas 1, lop 2, ripple 2
g.gql("MATCH (n:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN log(100) AS l").to_list() // #{"l": 4.605170185988092}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person {name: 'marko'}) RETURN ln(100) AS l").to_list() // #{"l": 4.605170185988092}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE (p)-[:created]->() RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // josh, marko, peter
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WHERE EXISTS { (p)-[:created]->() } RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // josh, marko, peter
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) UNWIND [p.age, p.age + 1] AS a RETURN a ORDER BY a").to_list() // #{"a": 29}, #{"a": 30}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) FOR a IN [p.age, p.age + 1] RETURN a ORDER BY a").to_list() // #{"a": 29}, #{"a": 30}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) CALL { WITH p MATCH (p)-[:knows]->(f) RETURN count(f) AS n } RETURN p.name AS name, n ORDER BY name").to_list() // josh 0, marko 2, peter 0, vadas 0
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) CALL (p) { MATCH (p)-[:knows]->(f) RETURN count(f) AS n } RETURN p.name AS name, n ORDER BY name").to_list() // josh 0, marko 2, peter 0, vadas 0
g.gql("CREATE (c:city:capital {name: 'Rome'}) RETURN labels(c) AS l").to_list() // #{"l": ["city", "capital"]}
g.gql("INSERT (c:city&capital {name: 'Rome'}) RETURN labels(c) AS l").to_list() // #{"l": ["city", "capital"]}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) SET p:admin:staff RETURN labels(p) AS l").to_list() // #{"l": ["person", "admin", "staff"]}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person {name: 'marko'}) SET p:admin, p:staff RETURN labels(p) AS l").to_list() // #{"l": ["person", "admin", "staff"]}
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) WHERE 27 <= n.age < 32 RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // marko, vadas
g.gql("MATCH (n:person) WHERE 27 <= n.age AND n.age < 32 RETURN n.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // marko, vadas
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[*2..1]->(b) RETURN b.name AS b").to_list() // [] (the empty interval) The importing WITH passes the variables on unchanged; a first WITH that renames, computes or filters (WITH p AS q, WITH p WHERE ..) is an ordinary WITH of a body that imports nothing, so reading p there is an error (write CALL (p) { .. }). A CALL { } without a scope clause and without that WITH imports nothing, as in Neo4j Cypher 25. Mind the Cypher *: -[*]-> starts at one edge, GQL's -[]->* at zero, so the GQL form also returns marko himself: g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[*]->(b) RETURN b.name AS b ORDER BY b").to_list() // josh, lop, lop, ripple, vadas
g.gql("MATCH (a {name: 'marko'})-[]->*(b) RETURN b.name AS b ORDER BY b").to_list() // josh, lop, lop, marko, ripple, vadas

464.body:
WITH is Cypher; GQL writes the same with its linear statements (LET, FILTER, and RETURN ... NEXT, UNION and NEXT ). gql() accepts WITH with Cypher's meaning: it passes on only its items, may take DISTINCT, aggregates, ORDER BY, SKIP / OFFSET, LIMIT and a WHERE (applied last), and a later clause continues from its columns ( The Supported Subset ). A WITH that only passes values on is the same as LET plus a narrower scope, and its WHERE the same as FILTER: g.gql("MATCH (p:person) WITH p, p.age / 10 AS d WHERE d = 3 RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // #{"name": "josh"}, #{"name": "peter"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) LET d = p.age / 10 FILTER d = 3 RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // #{"name": "josh"}, #{"name": "peter"}
g.gql("MATCH (p:person) RETURN p, p.age / 10 AS d NEXT FILTER d = 3 RETURN p.name AS name ORDER BY name").to_list() // #{"name": "josh"}, #{"name": "peter"}

