A rule with codec: "dedup" keeps one copy of every distinct value at its path in a table of the rule, and every element whose value is that text keeps only a reference to it. Reads stay plain and cost nothing extra (the text is read in place, nothing is decompressed); query results, change sets, exports and files never change, and the files keep their format (plain values; a load shares them again). // Every `symbol` vertex's `path` (a few hundred distinct file paths over 100,000 symbols).
g.define_compression(#{name: "symbol_path", label: "symbol", path: "path", codec: "dedup"});
g.compressions()[0] // compressed_values (the shared values), distinct_values, ... When it pays : long values repeated across many elements, such as file paths, URLs, addresses, category, status or type texts. Measured on a code knowledge graph: the path of its symbols took 1.17 MB of heap for 816 distinct values, about 40 KB deduplicated. When it does not : Short values. A string of at most 24 bytes (on 64-bit targets) is stored inline in the property slot and owns no heap: there is nothing to share, so a dedup rule never shares it, whatever its min_bytes (repo, kind, origin, short codes and names). The default min_bytes of a dedup rule is therefore 25. Mostly distinct values. Every distinct value costs its bytes plus 32 bytes (the reference counts of its shared copy and its slot in the table), so a path whose values rarely repeat (ids, descriptions, CVs) costs more than it saves. The estimate warns with high_cardinality; for long unique text use lz4. The rule's numbers: compressed_values counts the shared values (one per element), distinct_values the distinct texts among them, plain_bytes their plain size and stored_bytes each distinct text once plus 32 bytes for it; a reference itself costs nothing beyond the property slot a plain string occupies too. saved_bytes of an estimate is plain_bytes - stored_bytes. A text no element references any more (it was overwritten, its element dropped) leaves the table lazily: when the table has doubled since its last sweep, after a rolled-back transaction, on g.recompress(name) and on a load, so a table holds at most about twice its live values. g.memory_usage() reports deduped_values, deduped_plain_bytes and dedup_table_bytes (every shared text once, part of property_bytes). lz4 and dedup are one rule per element kind, label and path: redefining the rule with the other codec re-encodes its values in the same commit. Warning (0.2.0): Graphersal 0.2.0 does not know the codec dedup and reads a store that holds a dedup rule as damaged from the commit that defined it on (maintenance mode; its repair would drop those commits). Do not open such a store with 0.2.0, and never run graphersal store repair from 0.2.0 on it; use 0.2.1 or later.

