g.embed_property(#{label, from, to}) embeds the string property from (a name or a jpath(..)) of every element with label and stores the vector in its top-level property to. It is a job, not a traversal step, and returns a report: g.set_schema(g.infer_schema(), SchemaMode.open);
let report = g.embed_property(#{label: "person", from: "name", to: "emb"});
[report.embedded, report.units, report.schema_changed] // [4, 1, true] Option Default label (required) the label of the elements from (required) the text property, a name or a jpath(..) to (required) the vector property, a top-level name element "vertex" "edge" embeds edges embedder the host's default the registry name of the embedder only_missing true skip elements that have to already; false embeds every element again batch the embedder's max_batch elements per page (one embedder call and one unit each) The report counts embedded, skipped_no_text (no such property, null, or a text with nothing to embed), not_text (a value that is not a string: nothing is converted), skipped_changed and skipped_removed (see below), the committed units, whether the job declared the property in the schema (schema_changed), elapsed_ms, and the embedder and model that ran. How it runs. The job works in pages so that a slow embedder (a model in another process, a network service) never blocks the graph: it collects the ids of the elements to embed (a short read lock); per page it reads their texts (a short read lock), calls the embedder without any lock of the graph , so other queries and writers go on meanwhile, and then writes the page's vectors in one unit under the write lock; before writing an element it reads its text again: a text another writer changed since step 2 is skipped (skipped_changed), as is an element removed meanwhile (skipped_removed), so a stale vector is never stored. Each page is atomic; the job as a whole is not. When it fails (the embedder fails, a schema refuses a write, the run is cancelled), the pages before stay committed, the failing page is rolled back, and the error stops the script; since only_missing is the default, running the job again continues where it stopped. A text the embedder finds nothing in (an empty one, punctuation only) is skipped and counted, never a failure. Inside an open unit (an explicit transaction, and every script of the playground