Whether a query may write is decided when it is SAVED, from its body: the engine walks the body's Rhai syntax tree and infers its effects , the data writes its steps can make. A query with no effects is saved in the dialect graphersal-rhai/1 and only reads; a query with effects is saved in graphersal-rhai/2 and may write exactly those. Every save (define_query, define_queries, a replacement, the playground's editor) sets the dialect again, also back to /1 when the body no longer writes; moving or describing a query does not. g.define_query(#{name: "add_person", params: #{name: "string", age: "integer"}, body: "fn add_person(name, age) { g.add_v(\"person\").property(\"name\", name).property(\"age\", age).next() }"})
g.get_query("add_person")["dialect"]
// graphersal-rhai/2
g.get_query("add_person")["effects"].map(|e| e.text)
// "Create Data(vertex \"person\")"
// "Update Data(vertex \"person\")"
g.query("add_person", #{name: "ann", age: 41})
// v[7] An effect is a request as an authorizer sees it: an action (Create, Update, Delete) on Data, with the element kind and label the chain states before the writing step. The inference follows the same rules as the authorization check of a plan, so the run-time check can hold every write against it: Body Inferred add_v("person"), add_e("knows") Create on that label add_v(label) (a variable) Create on any vertex g.e().has_label("knows").drop(), out_e("knows").property(..) Delete / Update on knows edges g.v().has_label("person").property(..) Update on any vertex: a vertex has a label SET, so has_label("person") says nothing about its other labels a chain from a variable or __ (t.drop(), side_effect(__.property(..))) Delete / Update on any data merge_v(..) / merge_e(..) Create and Update on the kind g.query("other") the effects of other, when it writes g.gql("MATCH (p:person) INSERT (p)-[:owns]->(:pet)") the writes of the lowered GQL query: Create on pet vertices and owns edges g.gql("MATCH (p:person) SET p.age = 1") Update on any vertex, as for property() g.gql(text) with a computed text (a variable, a concatenation) Create, Update and Delete on any data: pass values as $parameters to keep a GQL read read-only a method of the same name on something else (m.drop() on a map) counted as a write The inference may claim more than a body writes, never less. What it cannot see, it refuses: a body that writes the schema, the catalog or files (set_schema, define_query, create_index, export_graphml, import_graphson, mark, ...) is refused when it is saved: a saved query writes data only; a body that writes and hides what it calls (Fn(..), call, curry, eval, import, or g.query(name) with a computed name) is refused when it is saved. Without writes such a body is saved as a read-only query, and whatever it reaches stays read-only. g.define_query(#{name: "q", params: #{n: "string"}, body: "fn q(n) { g.query(n); g.add_v(\"x\").next() }"})
// Error: Invalid body of saved query 'q': it writes data (Create Data(vertex "x")) and uses g.query(name) with a computed name, which hides what it calls, so its effects cannot be inferred; ... Effects are never stored. They are inferred again when a query is saved, listed or called, so a newer version of Graphersal may infer more or less for the same text; a call always uses the effects inferred now.

