g.v().has_label("person").has("name", __.to_lower().is(P.within("josh", "vadas"))).count().next();
g.v().has_label("person").has("name", __.to_lower().is(P.gte("p"))).values("name").to_list(); // The constant is compared AS GIVEN with the lowered value: write it in lower case.
g.v().has_label("person").has("name", __.to_lower().is("Marko")).count().next() // 0 What is served and what is not: Served: v()/e() with a folded has_label(..) (every label with a to_lower index on the key), followed in the run of pure filters by has(key, __.to_lower().is(P)) with P a value, P.eq, P.within or a range (P.gt/gte/lt/lte/between/inside/outside). .profile() shows [index: vertex person.name to_lower] on the start step; the has step stays in the plan and re-checks every candidate. Not served (a scan, the same result): any other child (__.is("x") without to_lower(), __.to_upper(), __.to_lower(Scope.local), more steps after is(..), P.neq, text predicates), where(__.values("name").to_lower().is(..)), a jpath key, a source without has_label. The constant is never lowered for you: is("Marko") matches nothing. Only strings are in the index; null and a missing key are not. Because to_lower() fails on any other value (an int64 name, a list), such a value must make the query fail as the scan does: while the index has skipped a non-null, non-string value (g.indexes() shows skipped), queries do not read it and scan, with the step's error. Remove or convert those values and the index is served again. A plain index and a to_lower index on the same key coexist: has("name", "Marko") reads the plain one, has("name", __.to_lower().is("marko")) the lowered one. A to_lower index never serves a plain has("name", "marko"). Creating one on a key the schema declares as a non-string type only (an integer) is refused with a message naming the transform; a union with string is fine ( below ). An unknown transform (written by a newer version) is kept byte for byte and listed as not served ( below ).

